VALUES
  ('cs_citigroup_operations', 'org_citigroup', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Travelers Group (1986–2007) The corporate logo of Travelers Inc. (1993–1998) prior to the merger with Citicorp Travelers Group, at the time of the merger, was a diverse group of financial concerns that had been brought together under CEO Sandy Weill . Its roots came from Commercial Credit, a subsidiary of Control Data Corporation that was taken private by Weill in November 1986 after taking charge of the company earlier that year. [1] Two years later, Weill mastered the buyout of Primerica Financial Services —a conglomerate that had already bought life insurance company A L Williams as well as brokerage firm Smith Barney . The new company took the Primerica name, and employed a " cross-selling " strategy such that each of the entities within the parent company aimed to sell each other''s services. Its non-financial businesses were spun off . In September 1992, Travelers Insurance , which had suffered from poor real estate investments [1] and sustained significant losses in the aftermath of Hurricane Andrew , [2] formed a strategic alliance with Primerica that would lead to its amalgamation into a single company in December 1993. With the acquisition, the group became Travelers Inc. Property &amp; casualty and life &amp; annuities underwriting capabilities were added to the business. Meanwhile, the distinctive Travelers red umbrella logo, which was also acquired in the deal, was applied to all the businesses within the newly named organization. During this period, Travelers acquired Shearson Lehman —a retail brokerage and asset management firm that was headed by Weill until 1985 [1] —and merged it with Smith Barney. Ownership of Salomon Brothers (1997–2003) In November 1997, Travelers Group (which had been renamed again in April 1995 when they merged with Aetna Property and Casualty, Inc.), acquired Salomon Brothers , a major bond dealer and bulge bracket investment bank , in a $9 billion (~$16.3 billion in 2024) transaction. [3] This deal complemented Travelers/ Smit', '["clm_wiki_citigroup_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 22. Meta Platforms (slug=meta-platforms, wiki='Meta Platforms')
