VALUES
  ('cs_comcast_leadership', 'org_comcast', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'American Cable Systems In 1963, Ralph J. Roberts in conjunction with his two business partners, Daniel Aaron [1] and Julian A. Brodsky , purchased American Cable Systems [2] as a corporate spin-off from its parent, Jerrold Electronics , for U.S. $500,000. At the time, American Cable was a small cable operator in Tupelo, Mississippi , with five channels and 12,000 customers. [3] In 1965, American Cable Systems purchased Storecast Corporation of America, a product placement supermarket specialist marketing firm. [4] In 1968, American Cable Systems purchased its first franchise of Muzak , a brand of background music played in retail stores. Storecast was a client of Muzak. [5] ^ body:not( ):not( ) David D. Kirkpatrick (February 24, 2003). "Daniel Aaron, 77, a Founder Of Comcast Cable Television" . The New York Times . Archived from the original on October 11, 2021 . Retrieved August 27, 2014 . ^ Farrell, Mike (June 19, 2015). "Comcastic: A Comcast Timeline" . Multichannel News . NewBay Media. Archived from the original on May 23, 2022 . Retrieved June 19, 2015 . ^ "A look at Comcast''s changes over the decades" . Seattle Times . Associated Press. January 18, 2011. Archived from the original on March 4, 2016 . Retrieved February 14, 2014 . ^ Ramachandran, Shalini; Hagey, Keach (June 21, 2018). "Two Titans'' Rocky Relationship Stands Between Comcast and Fox" . The Wall Street Journal . ISSN 0099-9660 . Archived from the original on October 23, 2018 . Retrieved October 23, 2018 . ^ James, Meg (June 26, 2014). "Comcast timeline: Rise of a giant" . Los Angeles Times . Archived from the original on November 21, 2018 . Retrieved October 23, 2018 .', '["clm_wiki_comcast_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
