VALUES
  ('cs_johnson-johnson_leadership', 'org_johnson-johnson', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   '1873–1885: Before Johnson &amp; Johnson Robert Wood Johnson began his professional training at age 16 as a pharmaceutical apprentice at an apothecary run by his mother''s cousin, James G. Wood, in Poughkeepsie, New York. [1] [2] &#58;&#8202;12&#8202; Johnson co-founded his own company with George J. Seabury in 1873. The New York-based Seabury &amp; Johnson became known for its medicated plasters. [3] &#58;&#8202;675&#8202; [2] &#58;&#8202;15&#8202; Robert Wood Johnson represented the company at the Centennial Exposition in 1876. [4] There he heard Joseph Lister ''s explanation of a new procedure: antiseptic surgery. [2] &#58;&#8202;31&#8202; Johnson parted ways with his business partner Seabury in 1885. [2] &#58;&#8202;38&#8202; ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Carbondale Area Native Founded Johnson &amp; Johnson" . The Times-Tribune . November 12, 2000. p. 129 &#8211; via Newspapers . ^ a b c d Foster LG (1999). The Gentleman Rebel . Lillian Press. ISBN 0966288203 . ^ Ingham JN (1983). Biographical Dictionary of American Business Leaders . Vol. 2. Greenwood Publishing Group. ISBN 0313239088 . ^ Rutkow I (June 2013). "Joseph Lister and his 1876 tour of America". Annals of Surgery . 257 (6): 1181– 7. doi : 10.1097/SLA.0b013e31826d9116 . PMID 23059499 . S2CID 389275 .', '["clm_wiki_johnson-johnson_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
