VALUES
  ('cs_general-electric_operations', 'org_general-electric', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Public company In 1896, General Electric was one of the original 12 companies listed on the newly formed Dow Jones Industrial Average , [1] where it remained a part of the index for 122 years, though not continuously. [2] In 1900, GE bought the U.S. business of Siemens-Halske . [3] &#58;&#8202;39&#8202; Carmen Miranda in a 1945 advertisement for a General Electric FM radio in The Saturday Evening Post In 1911, General Electric absorbed the National Electric Lamp Association (NELA) into its lighting business. GE established its lighting division headquarters at Nela Park in East Cleveland, Ohio . The lighting division has since remained in the same location. [4] During World War I , GE''s contributions to the American military included submarine motors, airplane compasses, winch drives for balloons, telephone switchboards , and bomb-release mechanisms. [3] &#58;&#8202;45&#8202; ^ body:not( ):not( ) Schaefer, Steve. "The First 12 Dow Components: Where Are They Now?" . Forbes . Archived from the original on January 5, 2018 . Retrieved January 5, 2018 . ^ "History of the Dow – Timeline of Companies" . Quasimodos . January 4, 1984. Archived from the original on April 22, 2012 . Retrieved April 23, 2012 . ^ a b Cohan, William D. (2022). Power failure: the rise and fall of an American icon . New York: Portfolio/Penguin. ISBN 978-0-593-08416-8 . ^ "Nela Park holiday lights, Hower House Museum display and recycle holiday lights: Home and Garden News" . cleveland . Archived from the original on June 21, 2017 . Retrieved March 24, 2017 .', '["clm_wiki_general-electric_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 51. Honeywell (slug=honeywell, wiki='Honeywell')
