VALUES
  ('cs_caterpillar_history', 'org_caterpillar', 'overview', 'History',
   'Etymology The origins of the word "caterpillar" date from the early 16th century. They derive from Middle English catirpel , catirpeller , probably an alteration of Old North French catepelose : cate , cat (from Latin cattus ) + pelose , hairy (from Latin pilōsus ). [1] The inchworm, or looper caterpillars from the family Geometridae are so named because of the way they move, appearing to measure the earth (the word geometrid means earth-measurer in Greek ); [2] the primary reason for this unusual locomotion is the elimination of nearly all the prolegs except the clasper on the terminal segment. Caterpillar nest of Thaumetopoea pityocampa on a Pinus halepensis branch A geometrid caterpillar or inchworm ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Caterpillar" . The American Heritage Dictionary of the English Language (4th ed.). Houghton Mifflin Company. 2004. Archived from the original on 2011-09-09 . Retrieved 2008-03-26 &#8211; via Dictionary . ^ "Geometridae" . merriam-webster . Merriam-Webster . Retrieved 2026-05-29 . .', '["clm_wiki_caterpillar_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   20, 1, 1, unixepoch());
