VALUES
  ('cs_tesla-energy_leadership', 'org_tesla-energy', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Tesla''s expansion into battery energy storage Giga Nevada , Tesla''s battery factory As Tesla, Inc. developed batteries for its electric car business, the company also started experimenting with using batteries for energy storage . Starting in 2012, Tesla installed prototype battery packs (later called the Powerpack) at the locations of a few industrial customers. [1] In November 2013, Tesla announced that it would build Giga Nevada , a factory to produce lithium-ion batteries. [2] [3] The Tesla Energy brand was introduced on April 30, 2015, as CEO Elon Musk announced that the company would apply its battery technology to a home energy storage system called the Powerwall. Five hundred pilot units were built at the Tesla Fremont Factory in California and installed during 2015. The Giga Nevada factory started limited production of Powerwalls and Powerpacks in the first quarter of 2016 [4] [5] using battery cells produced elsewhere, and began mass production of cells in January 2017. [6] This battery technology is not an innovation of what is available in the market but, according to Musk, the company offered a product that is easy to install, more attractive, less expensive and can also be easily maintained. [7] ^ body:not( ):not( ) Terdiman, Daniel (June 25, 2015). "How Tesla''s Commercial Batteries Have Changed The Future...For Winemakers?" . Fast Company . US. Archived from the original on September 24, 2022 . Retrieved May 29, 2016 . ^ "2013: Tesla Motors may make its own batteries" . Mercury News . November 15, 2014. Archived from the original on August 13, 2016 . Retrieved August 1, 2016 . ^ Savov, Vlad (November 6, 2013). "Tesla''s solution to battery shortages is to build its own ''giga factory'' " . theverge . Archived from the original on August 11, 2017 . Retrieved August 1, 2016 . ^ "Tesla Unveils Model 3" . Tesla. March 31, 2016. Archived from the original on March 9, 2021 . Retrieved April 1, 2016 . ^ Johnston, Adam (January 8, 2016). "Tesla Starts Off 2016 B', '["clm_wiki_tesla-energy_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
