VALUES
  ('cs_fidelity-investments_leadership', 'org_fidelity-investments', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Operations Fidelity operates a brokerage firm ; manages mutual funds , index funds , and wealth management ; and provides fund distribution, retirement services, securities execution and clearance, asset custody, and life insurance . It also offers brokerage clearing software products for financial services firms. [1] It also offers a donor-advised fund , Fidelity Charitable, for clients seeking to donate securities. It processes 5.5 million daily average trades and is one of the largest providers of 401(k) plans and manages employee benefit programs for more than 28,800 businesses. [1] Fidelity offers cryptocurrency trading, [2] [3] [4] [5] [6] is the sponsor of cryptocurrency ETFs, [7] [8] and has its own stablecoin , the Fidelity Digital Dollar (FIDD) on the Ethereum network. [9] The company also makes investments on its own account for the benefit of the founding family and its executives. [10] Investments have included Seaport Center and 2.5 million square feet of office space in Boston; [11] COLT Telecom Group ; [12] MetroRed; [13] Community Newspaper Company ; [14] Lanoga; [15] Hope Lumber; [15] ProBuild ; [16] and Boston Coach. [17] In 2016, an investigation by Reuters showed that Fidelity executives made investments in shares at a fraction of the price later paid by funds managed by Fidelity Investments; examples included buying shares in Alibaba Group for 7 cents each. Later purchases of shares by funds managed by Fidelity was seen as propping up the values of the shares owned by the founders, a possible conflict of interest . [18] [19] [20] Notable mutual funds Fidelity has three fund divisions: Equity (headquartered in Boston, Massachusetts ), High-Income (headquartered in Boston) and Fixed-Income (headquartered in Merrimack, New Hampshire ). [1] Fidelity Contrafund The company''s largest equity mutual fund is Fidelity Contrafund , which has $145 billion in assets, [21] making it the largest actively managed mutual fund in the U.S. William Danoff has mana', '["clm_wiki_fidelity-investments_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_fidelity-investments_operations', 'org_fidelity-investments', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'History The Fidelity Fund incorporated in Massachusetts on May 1, 1930, with Edward C. Johnson II serving as president. [1] The corporate structure changed in 1946 and became known as Fidelity Management &amp; Research (FMR). [2] In 1969, the company formed Fidelity International (FIL) to serve non-U.S. markets and subsequently spun it off in 1980 into an independent entity owned by its employees. [3] In 1982, the company began offering 401(k) products, [2] followed by computerized stock trading offerings in 1984. [2] In the 1990s, Fidelity launched its first commercial donor-advised fund , [4] became the first mutual fund company to offer a webpage , [5] and appointed Robert Pozen as CEO . [6] In September 2003, the company launched its first exchange-traded fund , the Fidelity Nasdaq Composite Index Tracking Stock Fund (ONEQ). [7] The company acquired Wealth Lab the following year, which was laterdecommissioned in 2020. [8] In 2007, the company changed its legal structure to a limited liability company , with FMR LLC becoming the owning entity. [9] Fidelity Ventures, its venture capital arm, was shut down in 2010. [10] In 2014, Abigail Johnson became president and CEO of Fidelity Investments (FMR) as well as chairman of Fidelity International (FIL). [11] She reduced dependence on open-ended mutual funds, instead having the company focus on financial advice, brokerage services, and venture capital . [12] In August 2018, Fidelity introduced mutual funds with no fees or expense ratios . [13] In September 2019, Fidelity completed the corporate spin-off of Eight Roads Ventures, its venture capital division. [14] In January 2023, Fidelity acquired Shoobx, a provider of automated equity management operations and financing software which was folded into Fidelity’s Stock Plan Services business. [15] In April 2026, Fidelity was named amongst “America’s Most Trustworthy Companies” by Newsweek . [16] ^ body:not( ):not( ) Porter, John Sherman, ed. (1944). Moody''s Manual of Inv', '["clm_wiki_fidelity-investments_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 112. Cox Enterprises (slug=cox-enterprises, wiki='Cox Enterprises')
