VALUES
  ('cs_snowflake_leadership', 'org_snowflake', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Nucleus In warmer clouds, an aerosol particle or "ice nucleus" must be present in (or in contact with) the droplet to act as a nucleus. The particles that make ice nuclei are very rare compared to nuclei upon which liquid cloud droplets form; however, it is not understood what makes them efficient. Clays, desert dust, and biological particles may be effective, [1] although to what extent is unclear. Artificial nuclei include particles of silver iodide and dry ice , and these are used to stimulate precipitation in cloud seeding . [2] Experiments show that "homogeneous" nucleation of cloud droplets only occurs at temperatures lower than −35 °C (−31 °F). [3] ^ body:not( ):not( ) Christner, Brent Q.; Morris, Cindy E.; Foreman, Christine M.; Cai, Rongman &amp; Sands, David C. (2007). "Ubiquity of Biological Ice Nucleators in Snowfall". Science . 319 (5867): 1214. Bibcode : 2008Sci...319.1214C . CiteSeerX 10.1.1.395.4918 . doi : 10.1126/science.1149757 . PMID 18309078 . S2CID 39398426 . : Cite uses deprecated parameter &#124;citeseerx= ( help ) ^ "Meteorology Glossary: Cloud seeding" . American Meteorological Society . 26 January 2012. Archived from the original on 22 December 2015 . Retrieved 2016-01-05 . ^ Mason, Basil John (1971). Physics of Clouds . Clarendon. ISBN 978-0-19-851603-3 .', '["clm_wiki_snowflake_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
