VALUES
  ('cs_snowflake_operations', 'org_snowflake', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Growth Scanning electron microscope image of rime frost on both ends of a capped column snowflake Once a water droplet has frozen as an ice nucleus, it grows in a supersaturated environment—wherein liquid moisture coexists with ice beyond its equilibrium point at temperatures below freezing. The droplet then grows by deposition of water molecules in the air (vapor) onto the ice crystal surface where they are collected. Because water droplets are so much more numerous than the ice crystals due to their sheer abundance, the crystals are able to grow to hundreds of micrometers or millimeters in size at the expense of the water droplets. This process is known as the Wegener–Bergeron–Findeisen process . [1] The corresponding depletion of water vapor causes the droplets to evaporate, meaning that the ice crystals grow at the droplets'' expense. These large crystals are an efficient source of precipitation, since they fall through the atmosphere due to their mass, and may collide and stick together in clusters, or aggregates. These aggregates are usually the type of ice particle that falls to the ground. [2] Guinness World Records lists the world''s largest aggregated snowflakes as those of January 1887 at Fort Keogh , Montana , which were claimed to be 38 cm (15 inches) wide—well outside the normally documented range of aggregated flakes of 7.5 or 10 cm (3 or 4 inches) in width. Single crystals the size of a dime (18 mm or 0.705 inches in diameter) have been observed. [3] &#91; page needed &#93; Snowflakes encapsulated in rime form balls known as graupel . [4] ^ body:not( ):not( ) Harper, Kristine (2007). Weather and climate: decade by decade . Twentieth-century science (illustrated ed.). Infobase Publishing. pp. 74– 75. ISBN 978-0-8160-5535-7 . ^ Klesius, M. (2007). "The Mystery of Snowflakes". National Geographic . 211 (1): 20. ISSN 0027-9358 . ^ Broad, William J. (2007-03-20). "Giant Snowflakes as Big as Frisbees? Could Be" . Science. The New York Times . Archived from t', '["clm_wiki_snowflake_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
