VALUES
  ('cs_atlassian_operations', 'org_atlassian', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Sales model Atlassian operates under the principle that "software should be bought, not sold". Instead of running a traditional sales team, they opted to build a self-service purchase experience. This was considered risky in the early 2000s, but the strategy worked better than expected when they awoke one morning to an order form from American Airlines in the fax machine. [1] While a majority of sales are made through their website, [2] Atlassian also runs a partner program where solution partners not only provide knowledge about Atlassian products but can also assist with product implementation and configuration depending on their partner classification. [3] [4] [5] ^ Cite error: The named reference auto1 was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ body:not( ):not( ) MacMillan, Douglas (8 April 2014). "Atlassian Valued at $3.3 Billion Selling Business Software Sans Salespeople" . Wall Street Journal Digits blog . Archived from the original on 2 February 2017 . Retrieved 25 January 2017 . ^ Atlassian. "Atlassian Partners: Receive Product Support" . Atlassian . Retrieved 16 June 2024 . ^ Player, Chris (13 October 2016). "Atlassian''s partner program comes of age" . arnnet .au . ^ Teal, Kelly (5 September 2019). "Atlassian ''Doubling Down'' on Cloud Means More Margin for Partners" . channelfutures .', '["clm_wiki_atlassian_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 155. Intuit (slug=intuit, wiki='Intuit')
