VALUES
  ('cs_wyndham-hotels-resorts_leadership', 'org_wyndham-hotels-resorts', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Early history The Wyndham hotel brand was created in 1981 in Dallas, Texas , by Trammell Crow , the president of Trammell Crow Company . [1] Business journalist Wyndham Robertson, who wrote a profile of Crow for Fortune magazine in the early 1970s, claimed that the hotel chain was named for her, saying, "He loved my name. I never got him to admit that he named [the hotels] for me in public but he did in private." [2] As the chain grew, it was acquired in 1998 by Patriot American Hospitality, later named Wyndham International . Blackstone Group bought Wyndham International in 2005, and later that year sold the Wyndham hotel brand to Cendant . [1] Cendant''s predecessor was Hospitality Franchise Systems (HFS), founded in 1990 as a vehicle to acquire hotel franchises. &#91; citation needed &#93; By 1995, it had acquired the Days Inn , Howard Johnson , Ramada , and Super 8 brands. HFS then expanded into other businesses, and, in 1997, merged with CUC International to form Cendant Corporation. &#91; citation needed &#93; ^ a b body:not( ):not( ) "Trammell Crow leaves lasting lodging imprint following death" . Hotel Business . February 7, 2009. Archived from the original on August 6, 2019 . Retrieved August 5, 2019 . ^ Post, Rose. "A writer''s view on rich, powerful in America" , Salisbury Post , August 3, 1986, page 1D.', '["clm_wiki_wyndham-hotels-resorts_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_wyndham-hotels-resorts_operations', 'org_wyndham-hotels-resorts', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Wyndham Hotel Group (2006–2018) In 2006, as part of a plan to break Cendant up into four separate companies, its hotel and timeshare businesses were spun off as Wyndham Worldwide . &#91; citation needed &#93; As a division of Wyndham Worldwide, Wyndham Hotel Group was composed of more than 9,000 hotels under 21 brands, located in over 75 countries, competing in brand markets ranging from economy to upscale. It had more than 40,000 employees around the world. [1] Lodging management services were provided to upscale properties through Wyndham Hotel Management. &#91; citation needed &#93; In 2008, Wyndham purchased U.S. Franchise Systems , owner of the Microtel and Hawthorn Suites brands, from Global Hyatt Corporation for $150 million. [2] Wyndham bought the Wisconsin-based Exel Inn chain in 2008 and converted all 22 of its properties to Wyndham brands. [3] As of 2008, Wyndham Destinations'' franchisees have formed an independent association called Owners 8 Association to present their concerns and grievances to Wyndham Destinations. [4] The association has argued that individual franchisees have currently limited role in Wyndham''s decision making. Wyndham Hotel Group''s Former CEO Eric A. Danziger, in an interview in 2009 with Asian Indian Hotel Owners magazine, emphasized that Wyndham maintains cordial relationships with franchisees. He also stated that each of Wyndham''s brands maintains an advisory board of individual property owners. [5] In 2010, Wyndham acquired the TRYP hotel brand from Sol Meliá Hotels &amp; Resorts of Spain. The brand, subsequently renamed Tryp by Wyndham, was positioned as a "select-service, midmarket" brand representing approximately 13,000 rooms and caters to business and leisure travelers in cosmopolitan cities including Madrid, Barcelona, Paris, Lisbon, New York, Frankfurt, Montevideo, Buenos Aires, São Paulo, Istanbul, etc. [6] The Federal Trade Commission (FTC) filed suit against Wyndham in June 2012 following a security breach that led to', '["clm_wiki_wyndham-hotels-resorts_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
