VALUES
  ('cs_take-two-interactive_operations', 'org_take-two-interactive', 'overview', 'Operations',
   '2001–2006: Further acquisitions and regulatory investigations Take-Two''s logo from 1999 to 2004 In February 2001, Brant stepped down as CEO of Take-Two to serve as its chairman , being replaced in the former role by Sumner. [1] [2] Around this time, the company had 658 employees. [3] With Rockstar, Take-Two invested into developments of sequels to Grand Theft Auto , including Grand Theft Auto 2 (1999), and Grand Theft Auto III (2001) along with sequels, Grand Theft Auto: Vice City (2002) and Grand Theft Auto: San Andreas (2004). By 2003, Take-Two had revenues exceeding US$1 billion . [4] In 2002, the Take-Two name was dropped as a publishing label when the short-lived publishing label Gotham Games was introduced. [5] In 2004, Take-Two paid US$22.3 million to Infogrames for the rights to the Civilization series . [6] [7] The company had also planned to acquire Vivendi Universal Games for $1 billion . [8] In 2005, Take-Two began a host of acquisitions, spending more than US$80 million buying game developers. [9] One of its largest acquisitions was for the development studios Visual Concepts and Kush Games from Sega for about US$32 million in January 2005. Both studios had been extensively behind several sports simulation games, branded through ESPN , and typically released updated versions each year, using a 2K brand to differentiate versions (such as ESPN NFL 2K5 ). In 2004, Take-Two had struck a deal with Sega to help publish these titles. Just prior to Take-Two''s acquisition, Electronic Arts announced it had secured the exclusive rights to create video games based on the National Football League (NFL) and a fifteen-year branding deal with ESPN , effectively killing Visual Concepts'' own NFL title and devaluing their other games. This decision led Sega to abandon the sports-game market. Take-Two acquired these studios and unique branding (including the 2K brand), as well as negotiated for exclusive rights with Major League Baseball for video games. [10] [11] A day af', '["clm_wiki_take-two-interactive_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 215. Epic Games (slug=epic-games, wiki='Epic Games')
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
