  ('cs_charter-communications_history', 'org_charter-communications', 'overview', 'History',
   'American telecommunications and mass media company Charter Communications, Inc. Headquarters in Stamford, Connecticut Type Public Traded as Nasdaq : CHTR (Class A) S&amp;P 500 component Industry Telecommunications Mass media Predecessors Time Warner Cable Bright House Networks Cox Communications (pending) Founded 1993 &#59;&#32;33 years ago ( 1993 ) in St. Louis , Missouri, U.S. Founders Barry Babcock Jerald Kent Howard Wood Headquarters Stamford, Connecticut , U.S. Area served 41 states [1] Key people Eric Zinterhofer ( chairman ) Chris Winfrey ( president and CEO ) Products Broadband Cable television Digital cable Digital telephone HDTV Home security Streaming television VoIP phone Internet security Brands Spectrum Revenue US$54.77 billion (2025) [2] Operating income US$12.91 billion (2025) [2] Net income US$4.987 billion (2025) [2] Total assets US$154.2 billion (2025) [2] Total equity US$16.05 billion (2025) [2] Owners Liberty Media (23.3%) [3] Advance Publications (13%) [4] Berkshire Hathaway (2.33%) Number of employees 91,900 (2025) [2] ASN body:not( ):not( ) 7843 Website corporate Evolution of Spectrum 1962 Cox Enterprises acquires several cable companies to establish what later becomes Cox Communications 1968 Tele-Communications, Inc. is founded and later becomes the largest cable company in the U.S for nearly twenty years 1973 Warner Communications establishes a cable division and eventually launches the QUBE system , which heavily popularized cable menus 1979 American Express and Warner Cable create the MTV Networks joint venture which launches multiple cable channels 1982 Time Inc. grows to become a major cable operator after acquiring American Television and Communications (ATC) 1990 Warner Communications and Time Inc. merge to form Time Warner as the world''s largest media company 1992 Warner Cable and ATC are consolidated into Time Warner Cable , which forms the second-largest cable company in the U.S 1993 Charter Communications is founded and merges with Marcus Cable in 1997 1994 Time Warner and Advance/Newhouse form a joint venture that later becomes Bright House Networks . 2000 AT&amp;T acquires Tele-Communications Inc. along with MediaOne Group , and becomes the largest cable operator in the U.S 2001 Heavy debt leads to AT&amp;T to sell its cable assets to Comcast while Liberty Media is spun off to comply with regulations 2007 After acquiring the bankrupt Adelphia Communications , Time Warner Cable succeeds it as a public company 2014 Comca', '["clm_wiki_charter-communications_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
