VALUES
  ('cs_ecolab_leadership', 'org_ecolab', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   '1923–1986 The company was founded in 1923 as Economics Laboratory by Merritt J. "M.J." Osborn. The company''s first product was Absorbit, designed to quickly clean carpets in hotel rooms. It was followed by Soilax, a dishwasher soap. Its first overseas subsidiary, Soilax AB, was established in Sweden in 1956. [1] EL became a public company in 1957. [1] [2] It acquired Klenzade in 1961 and the Magnus Company in 1964, giving the company access to Magnus''s industrial specialty businesses – including pulp and paper , metalworking , transportation , and petrochemical processing. [1] [2] In 1972, the company acquired Raburn Products, maker of dish machine racks and dish room carts. [3] In February 1980, it acquired Apollo Technologies, a major producer of conditioning agents for electrostatic precipitators , for $71.2 million; it was shut down in 1983 with a $42 million loss. [4] In 1984, Ecolab acquired Lystads, thereby launching its pest elimination division. [5] ^ a b c body:not( ):not( ) "Ecolab" . Fundinguniverse . Retrieved 2026-07-30 . ^ a b Kirkland, Catherine (October 24, 2017). "How St. Paul''s Ecolab Got Started from a Stain on the Carpet" . Twin Cities Business . Retrieved 2026-07-30 . ^ Cite error: The named reference bcc was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ Mcllvaine, Robert W. (March 1983). "Market Trends for Air Pollution Control Equipment" . Journal of the Air Pollution Control Association . ^ Lupo, Lisa (March 6, 2018). "Ecolab Expands" . PCT Magazine . Retrieved 2026-07-30 .', '["clm_wiki_ecolab_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
