   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_owens-corning_history', 'org_owens-corning', 'overview', 'History',
   'American manufacturer of building materials Not to be confused with Corning Inc. or O-I Glass . Owens Corning Type Public Traded as NYSE : OC S&amp;P 400 component Russell 1000 component Industry General Building Materials, Composites Founded 1938 &#59;&#32;88 years ago ( 1938 ) in Toledo, Ohio , US Headquarters Toledo, Ohio, US Key people Brian Chambers ( CEO ) Products Insulating systems Composite solutions Roofing and asphalt Other building materials and services Revenue US$10.98 billion (2024) Operating income US$647 million (2024) Net income US$1.127 billion (2024) Total assets US$14.08 billion (2024) Total equity US$5.077 billion (2024) Number of employees c. &#8201;25,000 (2024) Website www /en-us Footnotes [1] [2] Owens Corning is an American company that develops and produces insulation, roofing, and fiberglass composites and related products. It is the world''s largest manufacturer of fiberglass composites. [3] [4] It was formed in 1935 as a partnership between two major American glassworks , Corning Glass Works and Owens-Illinois . The company employs approximately 19,000 people around the world. [5] [6] Owens Corning has been a Fortune 500 company every year since the list was created in 1955. [6] [7] The Pink Panther is the company''s mascot and appears in most of their advertisements. ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Owens Corning 2024 Annual Report (Form 10-K)" . U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission . February 24, 2024. ^ "Brian Chambers" . Media.OwensCorning . Owens Corning . Retrieved July 23, 2019 . ^ Collins, Glenn (November 12, 1994). "Europe''s Insulation Market Is Target of Owens-Corning" . The New York Times . Archived from the original on March 8, 2016 . Retrieved October 19, 2015 . ^ "Owens Corning" . November 27, 2016. Archived from the original on October 7, 2022 . Retrieved August 14, 2020 . ^ Redden, Susan (July 10, 2015). "Governor, local officials, welcome Owens Corning division to Joplin" . The Joplin Globe . Retrieved October 19, 2015 . ^ a b Linkhorn, Tyrel (October 27, 2013). "A marriage of two companies, Owens Corning celebrates 75th anniversary" . The Blade . Retrieved October 19, 2015 . ^ "1955 Full list" . Fortune . Archived from the original on August 23, 2013 . Retrieved October 19, 2015 .', '["clm_wiki_owens-corning_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   20, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_owens-corning_leadership', 'org_owens-corning', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   '1935–1980 Fiberglass production in 1942 Owens-Corning Fiberglas Company was formed in 1935 through a joint venture of Owens-Illinois and Corning Glass Works . [1] [2] It became a separate company in 1938 with its headquarters established in Toledo, Ohio. [3] [4] In 1938, the company sales reached $2.6 million. [5] The company held its initial public offering on the New York Stock Exchange in 1952. [6] In 1955, Owens-Corning purchased land for a research and testing facility near Granville, Ohio . [5] Also in 1955, Owens-Corning made the first Fortune 500 company list. The company has been on the Fortune 500 list every year since its creation. [3] [7] In 1965, Owens-Corning Fiberglas Europe was formed. In 1966, Owens-Corning established a partnership with Armstrong Rubber Co. to produce fiberglass-reinforced automobile tires. By 1971, Owens-Corning''s annual revenue was over $500 million. In 1974, the company opened a temporary plant to produce insulation for the Trans-Alaska Pipeline System . In 1977, Owens-Corning acquired Frye Roofing and began production of fiberglass mat to replace traditional paper mat used in roofing. [8] Owens-Corning sales surpassed $1 billion in 1976, and sales were over $2 billion by 1979. In 1980, Owens Corning entered into a long-standing agreement with United Artists to use the Pink Panther as its brand mascot. "He has become an important part of our brand," said Christian Nolte, director of strategic marketing for the manufacturer of building materials and fiberglass. "The Pink Panther is as relevant today as he was over 25 years ago. He brings our trademark color pink to life." [9] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "The Great Depression" (PDF) . Toledo Library. Archived from the original (PDF) on March 6, 2016 . Retrieved October 19, 2015 . ^ Keller, Michael E. (1998). The Graham Legacy: Graham-Paige to 1932 . Turner Publishing Company. ^ a b Cite error: The named reference Harold was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ "Fiber Glass P', '["clm_wiki_owens-corning_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
