VALUES
  ('cs_owens-corning_operations', 'org_owens-corning', 'overview', 'Operations',
   '1981–present Owens Corning headquarters In 1985, Owens-Corning acquired Aerospace and Strategic Metals Group in Newport Beach, California for $415 million from Armco Inc. [1] [2] The color PINK was trademarked through Owens-Corning in 1986, making it the first company to trademark a color. [3] The company opened an Asia/Pacific division in 1993. [4] In May 1994, Owens-Corning acquired UC Industries, which produced Foamular polystyrene insulation, as a wholly owned subsidiary. [5] In 1996, the company changed its name to Owens Corning. In May 1997, Owens Corning acquired Fibreboard Corporation, a vinyl siding and other industrial material manufacturer, [6] which became a wholly owned subsidiary of Owens Corning. [7] The company was ordered to pay $5 million to an asbestos victim in 1997, making it the highest jury verdict in the history of the United States for a single non-malignant asbestos case. [8] In 1999, a jury in federal district court in Florida awarded $1.8 million compensatory damages and $31 million punitive damages against the company for asbestos products. [9] The company filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy in 2000. In 2002, Owens Corning removed its shares from the New York Stock Exchange . In 2006, the company was listed on the New York Stock Exchange again under ticker "OC." In 2007, Owens Corning announced a joint venture with Saint-Gobain named OCV Reinforcements. [10] Owens Corning partnered with DuPont in 2011 to create a steel and foam energy reduction (SAFER) barrier around auto racetracks. [7] In July 2015, the company announced it would be moving a manufacturing center for mineral wool insulation into an empty plant west of Joplin, Missouri . [11] [12] In May 2024 the company completed the acquisition of Masonite International, a provider of interior and exterior door systems, for $3.9B. [13] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Orange County" . Los Angeles Times . September 27, 1985 . Retrieved October 19, 2015 . ^ Horovitz, Bruce (August 17, 1985). "Owens-Cor', '["clm_wiki_owens-corning_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 288. Vulcan Materials (slug=vulcan-materials, wiki='Vulcan Materials')
