VALUES
  ('cs_texas-instruments_operations', 'org_texas-instruments', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Semiconductors In early 1952, Texas Instruments purchased a patent license from Western Electric , the manufacturing arm of AT&amp;T, to produce germanium transistors for US$25,000, beginning production by the end of the year. Haggerty brought Gordon Teal to the company due to his expertise in growing semiconductor crystals while at Bell Telephone Laboratories . Teal''s first assignment was to direct TI''s research laboratory. At the end of 1952, Texas Instruments announced that it had expanded to 2,000 employees and $17 million in sales. [1] Among his new hires was Willis Adcock , who joined TI early in 1953. Adcock, who like Teal was a physical chemist , began leading a small research group focused on the task of fabricating grown-junction, silicon , single-crystal, small-signal transistors. [2] Adcock later became the first TI Principal Fellow. [3] First silicon transistor and integrated circuits Transistorized " logic " chip, an integrated circuit produced by TI In January 1954, Morris Tanenbaum at Bell Telephone Laboratories created the first workable silicon transistor. [2] This work was reported in the spring of 1954, at the IRE off-the-record conference on solid-state devices, and was later published in the Journal of Applied Physics . Working independently in April 1954, Gordon Teal at TI created the first commercial silicon transistor and tested it on April 14, 1954. On May 10, 1954, at the Institute of Radio Engineers National Conference on Airborne Electronics in Dayton, Ohio, Teal presented a paper: "Some Recent Developments in Silicon and Germanium Materials and Devices". [4] In 1954, Texas Instruments designed and manufactured the first transistor radio . The Regency TR-1 used germanium transistors, as silicon transistors were much more expensive at the time. This was an effort by Haggerty to increase market demand for transistors. Jack Kilby , an employee at TI, invented the integrated circuit in 1958. [5] Kilby recorded his initial ideas concerning th', '["clm_wiki_texas-instruments_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 299. Qualcomm (slug=qualcomm, wiki='Qualcomm')
