VALUES
  ('cs_sysco_operations', 'org_sysco', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Acquisitions and expansion In 1988, Sysco achieved nationwide coverage through its acquisition of the food distributor CFS Continental . [1] By 1996, Sysco was the third-largest company in Houston and had over 30,000 employees. [2] Sysco acquired Newport Meat in 1999, [3] which at the time had sales of approximately $100 million per year. This was the company''s first acquisition of a California company. [4] Sysco''s annual sales in 1995 were $12 billion (equivalent to $22 billion in 2024), increasing by 2000 to $19 billion (equivalent to $33 billion in 2024). [1] In 2002, Sysco expanded into the Canadian market by purchasing SERCA Foodservices for $278 million. [5] SERCA''s business was similar to Sysco''s, with the Canadian company supplying food products and foodservice supplies to approximately 80,000 customers at the time of the acquisition. [5] The following year, Sysco acquired Asian Foods, which was then the largest Asian food distribution company in North America. [6] In 2009, Sysco made its first acquisition outside of North America, buying the Irish food distributor Pallas Foods. [7] [8] Further expanding its footprint in Ireland, Sysco purchased the food distributor Crossgar Foodservice for an undisclosed amount in 2012. [9] In December 2012, Sysco acquired American companies Appert''s Foodservice, Buchy Food Service and Central Seafood Company, and the Distagro food service division of Canadian supermarket chain Metro-Richelieu Inc. [10] On December 9, 2013, Sysco announced they were planning to acquire US Foods , their closest competitor, for a total of $3.5 billion. [11] However, on June 23, 2015, US Federal Judge Amit Mehta ruled that the combined Sysco-US Foods would control 75% of the U.S. food service industry and would stifle competition. [12] As a result, on June 29, 2015, Sysco terminated its merger with US Foods. [13] In February 2016, Sysco announced that it was purchasing the British company Brakes for $3.1 billion. [14] At the time, Brakes was s', '["clm_wiki_sysco_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
