VALUES
  ('cs_foot-locker_operations', 'org_foot-locker', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Rebranding as Foot Locker As the "Foot Locker" brand had become the Woolworth/Venator company''s top performing line, on November 2, 2001, Venator changed its name to Foot Locker, Inc. [1] At that time, Serra became CEO of the company. In 2004, he was also named chairman. In the early 2000s, the majority of Foot Locker stores were renovated and standardized. [2] On November 19, 2004, Foot Locker announced that its quarterly profit rose 19 percent, helped by stronger sales. [3] In 2004, Foot Locker acquired the Footaction USA brand and approximately 350 stores from Footstar for $350 million (~$555 million in 2024). [4] On April 14, 2004, Foot Locker Inc. announced that it agreed to buy about 350 Footaction stores from bankrupt Footstar Inc. for $160 million (~$254 million in 2024) to expand in urban areas. [5] On January 10, 2005, the company announced that Nick Grayston was promoted to President and Chief Executive Officer of its Foot Locker U.S. division, succeeding Tim Finn, who retired from the company. [6] In 2007, Foot Locker joined with schoolPAX [7] to launch the Foot Locker School Rewards Program, [8] designed to provide charitable donations to schools who sign up and shop at Foot Locker with a custom-coded key tag or school code. Also that year, Foot Locker attempted a revival of the Footquarters name and store format, but put an end to the idea afte only 3 months. [9] In 2008, Foot Locker purchased CCS, a skateboarding equipment retailer, from Alloy for $103 million in cash. [10] In August 2009, Ken Hicks succeeded Matt Serra as CEO. [2] In 2011, Foot Locker joined DoSomething.Org for the Foot Locker Scholar Athletes program, which honors high school athletes for demonstrating academic excellence and flexing their hearts on their sports teams and in their communities. On June 26, 2012, Foot Locker celebrated the 100th anniversary of the first stock offering made by its predecessor, the F. W. Woolworth Company, on the New York Stock Exchange by ringing the C', '["clm_wiki_foot-locker_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
