   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_kennametal_operations', 'org_kennametal', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'History Kennametal was founded in 1938 by Philip M. McKenna in the Latrobe, Pennsylvania area. [1] [2] The company evolved from Vanadium Alloys Steel Company (VASCO), founded in 1910 by the McKenna family with its headquarters in Latrobe, Pennsylvania . While research director of VASCO, metallurgist Philip McKenna developed and received a patent for a tungsten-titanium carbide composition. McKenna formed Kennametal, Inc. for the purpose of marketing this alloy. [3] The company''s original flagship product, known as "Kennametal", was introduced in the late 1930s. It was described as "much harder than the hardest tool steel," which enabled high-rate steel cutting not possible previously. [4] In July 1940, Kennametal of Canada Ltd. was organized for the purpose of "manufacturing Kennametal, the new steel cutting carbide, for Canada and British dominions." [5] In November 1940, United States Steel Export Company reached an agreement to sell Kennametal tools, tool blanks, drawing dies and other Kennametal products in foreign markets. [6] During World War II, more than 50% of the artillery shells produced in the United States were machined with the Kennametal product. [7] During this period the company developed a new type of anti-tank projectile made of tungsten with a hard carbide core. [8] The company has expanded through acquisitions, [9] [10] including the purchase of Widia Group (2002, 188 million Euros) [11] Extrude Hone Corp. (2005, $137 million) [12] and Allegheny Technologies'' tungsten materials unit (2013, $605 million). [13] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Kennametal Inc: Company and People" . Barron''s . Retrieved 11 November 2024 . ^ "About Us" . www . Archived from the original on 2015-12-15. ^ Derdak, Thomas. "Kennametal, Inc" . Encyclopedia . Retrieved 10 November 2024 . ^ "Kennametal Being Used in Many Ways" . Pittsburgh Post-Gazette. 4 September 1946 . Retrieved 11 November 2024 . ^ "Kennametal of Canada Organized with Offices in Hamilton, Ontario" . Toronto Star a', '["clm_wiki_kennametal_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
