VALUES
  ('cs_jacobs-solutions_operations', 'org_jacobs-solutions', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Controversies Kingston coal ash cleanup Main article: Kingston Fossil Plant coal fly ash slurry spill The Kingston Fossil Plant coal fly ash slurry spill was an environmental and industrial disaster which occurred on Monday December 22, 2008, when a dike ruptured at a coal ash pond at the Tennessee Valley Authority ''s Kingston Fossil Plant in Harriman, Tennessee releasing 1.1 billion U.S. gallons (4.2 million cubic meters) of coal fly ash slurry . [1] The Tennessee Valley Authority hired Jacobs Engineering to clean up the spill. [2] In the years after the spill at the cleanup site, a number of workers suffered health effects. [3] As early as 2012, workers began to report illnesses which they believed were caused by the cleanup, [3] and by the ten year anniversary of the event, hundreds of workers had been sickened and more than 30 had died. [4] In May 2023, it was reported that more than 50 workers had died and 150 were sick. [5] In 2013, 50 workers and their families filed a lawsuit against contractor Jacobs Engineering. [3] They were represented by Knoxville lawyer James K. Scott and the lawsuit was dismissed by judge Thomas A. Varlan, chief justice for the U.S. District Court for the Eastern District of Tennessee in 2014. [1] This ruling was reversed by the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit after evidence was discovered that Jacobs Engineering had misled the workers about the dangers of coal ash. A federal jury ruled in favor of the workers seeking compensation in November 2018. The ruling held that Jacobs Engineering had failed to keep the workers safe from environmental hazards, and had misled them about the dangers of coal ash, mainly by claiming that extra protective equipment, such as masks and protective clothing, was unnecessary. [6] [7] In a phase two of the trial, the Kingston cleanup workers will be able to seek damages. [3] In April 2020, 52 workers rejected a $10 million settlement offered by Jacobs Engineering. [8] Hinkley Point See also: H', '["clm_wiki_jacobs-solutions_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
