VALUES
  ('cs_aes_leadership', 'org_aes', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   '20th century U.S. Secretary of State Marco Rubio and AES CEO &amp; President Andres Gluski at the AES liquefied natural gas facility in Colón, Panama , on February 2, 2025 The company was founded on January 28, 1981, as Applied Energy Services [1] by Roger Sant and Dennis Bakke, two appointees of the Federal Energy Administration under president Richard Nixon . The company was initially a consulting firm; it became AES Corporation, which went public in 1991. Sant was chairman, CEO, and president and Bakke was executive vice president until assuming the position of president in 1987. Bakke later became the company''s CEO in 1994, serving for eight years until his resignation in 2002 in the midst of a liquidity crisis that followed the collapse of the energy giant Enron . [2] [3] The company''s efforts to modernize the electricity grid in Tbilisi, Georgia, through its acquisition of Telasi, were documented in the feature documentary Power Trip , directed by Paul Devlin . [4] [5] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "AES - Frequently Asked Questions" . Investor . Archived from the original on October 22, 2006 . Retrieved October 7, 2012 . ^ Gross, Daniel (March 11, 2003). "When Bad Things Happen to Good Companies" . Slate . ISSN 1091-2339 . ^ "Ex-AES CEO now puts his energy in education" . NH Business Review . November 25, 2005. ^ "Power Trip documentary overview" . ITVS . Retrieved December 12, 2025 . ^ "THE ELECTRIC COMPANY" . Filmmaker Magazine . Retrieved December 15, 2025 .', '["clm_wiki_aes_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
