VALUES
  ('cs_cadence-design-systems_operations', 'org_cadence-design-systems', 'overview', 'Operations',
   '2020–2025 Cadence had 9,300 employees and annual revenue of $3 billion in 2021. [1] Most of its revenue came from licensing its software and intellectual property. [2] In April 2021, following a Washington Post report on the use of Cadence and Synopsys technology in the People''s Liberation Army ''s military-civil fusion efforts, [3] U.S. legislators Michael McCaul and Tom Cotton requested that the United States Department of Commerce tighten controls on the sales of semiconductor manufacturing software. [4] [5] On December 15, 2021, Anirudh Devgan assumed the role of Cadence president &amp; CEO, after having been named Cadence president in 2017. Lip-Bu Tan retired as CEO and became executive chairman [6] and left this position and the board in May 2023. In 2021, Cadence launched an artificial intelligence platform to streamline processor development. [7] Although most of Cadence''s customers for decades were "traditional semiconductor firms," around 40% of Cadence''s revenue by 2022 came from customers who were "systems" oriented, or seeking products tailored for various industries that utilized chips in a central role. Cadence was also increasingly designing customized chips for clients and having them manufactured by third parties such as Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing , a practice which had become more popular in the face of worldwide chip shortages and shipping issues, according to Reuters . [8] By late 2022, Cadence had clients such as Tesla and Apple Inc. [8] Cadence acquired OpenEye Scientific Software for $500 million in September 2022, rebranding the company OpenEye Cadence Molecular Sciences and making it into a business unit. [1] OpenEye signed Pfizer as a software client in October 2023. [9] Cadence purchased several businesses from Rambus in 2023. [10] In February 2024, Cadence "quietly stepped into the supercomputer business," according to TechRadar , when it unveiled the M1, its own supercomputer designed to run computational fluid dynamics (CFD) whi', '["clm_wiki_cadence-design-systems_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
