VALUES
  ('cs_cboe-global-markets_operations', 'org_cboe-global-markets', 'overview', 'Operations',
   '1973: Launch Founded by the Chicago Board of Trade in 1973 and member-owned for several decades, the Chicago Board Options Exchange was the first exchange to list standardized, exchange-traded stock options. Its first day of trading was on April 26, 1973, in celebration of the 125th birthday of the Chicago Board of Trade. [1] [2] Trading was conducted in the former CBOT smoking lounge. [3] In its first full month of operation, 34,599 contracts were traded. By 1976, the monthly volume of trades had increased to 1.5 million. [4] ^ body:not( ):not( ) Baker, Howard. "Cboe Marks Golden Anniversary" (PDF) . Museum of American Finance . ^ Maidenberg, H. J. (April 27, 1973). "Trading in Options Opens On New Chicago Board" . The New York Times . ^ Roeder, David (October 13, 2020). "Joseph Sullivan III, founding president of Chicago Board Options Exchange, dies at 82" . Chicago Sun Times . ^ Finnerty, Joseph E. (1978). "The Chicago board options exchange and market efficiency" . Journal of Financial and Quantitative Analysis . 13 (1): 29– 38. doi : 10.2307/2330518 . hdl : 2027.42/35606 . JSTOR 2330518 .', '["clm_wiki_cboe-global-markets_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
