VALUES
  ('cs_amcor_leadership', 'org_amcor', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   '2000s The printing paper divisions were spun-off into a new company, named Paperlinx , in April 2000. This demerger was intended to enable Amcor to focus on global packaging. [1] Amcor''s 45 percent interest in Kimberly-Clark Australia , a tissue and personal-care products company, was divested in June 2002. [2] [3] Factory entrance of Amcor Flexibles Tscheulin Rothal GmbH in Teningen, Germany Amcor Rigid Plastics plant in Manchester, Michigan , United States In July 2002, Amcor acquired the rigid packaging and closures businesses of Schmalbach-Lubeca, headquartered in Ratingen , Germany . [2] This acquisition, at a cost of around $2.875 billion, made Amcor the largest manufacturer of PET ( Polyethylene terephthalate ) containers, globally. [4] In 2007, Amcor sold its European PET business to La Seda, a Spain -based manufacturer. [5] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Group History" . Paperlinx . Archived from the original on 25 July 2011 . Retrieved 10 July 2011 . ^ a b "Amcor acquires Schmalbach-Lubeca operations" . Packaging World . Archived from the original on 13 September 2023 . Retrieved 5 May 2018 . ^ "Kimberly-Clark Completes Acquisition of K–C Australia" . Kimberly-Clark . Archived from the original on 22 May 2018 . Retrieved 5 May 2018 . ^ "Eastern Europeans Balk at PET Barrier Bottles" . plasticstoday . 1 July 2003. Archived from the original on 13 September 2023 . Retrieved 19 August 2020 . ^ "Amcor sells Euro business for $676m" . The Sydney Morning Herald . 2 July 2007. Archived from the original on 26 September 2017 . Retrieved 26 September 2017 .', '["clm_wiki_amcor_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
