VALUES
  ('cs_prologis_history', 'org_prologis', 'overview', 'History',
   'American real estate company Prologis, Inc. Headquarters at Pier 1 in San Francisco Type Public Traded as NYSE : PLD S&amp;P 500 component Industry Real estate Predecessor AMB Property Corporation Founded 1983 &#59;&#32;43 years ago ( 1983 ) Founders Hamid Moghadam Doug Abbey T. Robert Burke Headquarters Pier 1 , San Francisco , California , United States Area served North America South America Europe Asia Key people Hamid Moghadam, Executive chairman [1] Dan Letter, CEO [2] Carter H. Andrus, COO [1] Tim Arndt, CFO [1] Products Industrial real estate Revenue US$ 8.79 billion (2025) [3] Operating income US$4.36 billion (2025) [3] Net income US$3.32 billion (2025) [3] AUM US$230 billion (2025) [4] Total assets US$98.72 billion (2025) [3] Total equity US$57.75 billion (2025) [3] Number of employees 2,802 (2025) [3] Website prologis Prologis, Inc. is a real estate investment trust headquartered in San Francisco, California that invests in logistics facilities. [5] The company was formed through the merger of AMB Property Corporation and Prologis in June 2011, which made Prologis the largest industrial real estate company in the world. [6] [7] As of 2025, &#91;update&#93; the company operates more than 15,000 land acres and over 6,000 buildings [8] comprising about 1.3 billion square feet in 20 countries [9] across North America , Latin America , Europe , and Asia . [10] According to The Economist , its business strategy is focused on warehouses that are located close to huge urban areas where land is scarce. [11] It serves about 6,600 tenants. [12] Prologis began to expand its non-real estate business, Essentials, in 2018, offering customers solar power, racking systems, forklifts, generators, [13] EV charging infrastructure , [14] and other logistics tech equipment for purchase. ^ a b c body:not( ):not( ) "Prologis Inc" . The Wall Street Journal . Retrieved August 5, 2026 . ^ [ https://www /about/leadership-board-directors "Leadership and Board of Directors"] Prologis. access-date=August 5, 2026}} ^ a b c d e f "Prologis Financial Statements 2011-2026 PLD" . macrotrends . Retrieved August 5, 2026 . ^ "Prologis Reports Q4 and Full-Year 2025 Financial Results" . theglobeandmail . Retrieved August 5, 2026 . ^ "Prologis, Inc. 2021 Form 10-K Annual Report" . SEC . U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission . 9 February 2022. ^ Morris, Keiko (May 30, 2017). "Industrial Park''s Owner Bringing in Restaurants, Retail as Amenities for Workers" . The Wall Street Journal . [', '["clm_wiki_prologis_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   20, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_prologis_leadership', 'org_prologis', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   '1983 founding and early history In 1983, Hamid Moghadam and Doug Abbey founded Abbey, Moghadam and Company, [1] an investment manager serving institutions. [2] T. Robert Burke joined the company in 1984, establishing AMB Property Corporation, investors in office, industrial and community shopping centers on behalf of large institutional investors . [1] During the savings and loan crisis , the company avoided significant financial repercussions by investing in industrial parks and shopping centers, and began to exit the office market in 1987. [1] AMB launched its first private equity fund in 1989, which focused on industrial and retail properties. [2] In late 1997, [2] AMB became a public company via an initial public offering , with more than US$2.8 billion under management. [1] In 1999, the company sold its retail portfolio to focus solely on the industrial sector, then made its first overseas investment in 2002, developing a facility for Procter &amp; Gamble . [2] That year, AMB initiated an international expansion program focused on buying and developing distribution facilities near global trade hubs, particularly in growth markets such as Latin America , Asia , [3] and Europe. [1] AMB added an internal development division in 2004 and, by 2011, was expanding its operations in China and Brazil. [2] ^ a b c d e body:not( ):not( ) "History" . Prologis. ^ a b c d e Cite error: The named reference robaton was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ Cite error: The named reference default was invoked but never defined (see the help page ).', '["clm_wiki_prologis_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_prologis_operations', 'org_prologis', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Creation of Prologis legacy company SCI and international expansion Real Estate entrepreneur William Sanders formed Security Capital Industrial Trust (SCI), a legacy company of Prologis, [1] in 1991, and it became a public company via an IPO in 1994. [2] ProLogis, Inc. (then with a capitalized L) was incorporated on November 24, 1997 under the leadership of K. Dane Brooksher, a former KPMG senior executive. [3] SCI officially changed its name to ProLogis Trust in July 1998, then active in 84 markets in 12 countries, with a market capitalization of nearly $5 billion. [4] In November 1998, ProLogis Trust acquired Meridian Industrial Trust for $862.5 million in stock, and was the largest owner of industrial and warehouse properties in the U.S. [5] ProLogis Trust formed its first property funds in 1999 and entered the Japanese market in 2001, the same year it shortened its name to ProLogis. In 2003, ProLogis was added to the S&amp;P 500 Index and entered the Chinese market, forming its first joint venture in China with Suzhou Logistics Center Co. Ltd. [6] In 2004, the company acquired Keystone Industrial Trust for $1.6 billion. [7] Brooksher retired as chairman and CEO on December 31, 2004. [8] In 2005, Prologis was headquartered in Aurora, Colorado , and acquired Catellus Development Corporation for $3.6 billion, [9] later selling a portfolio of those assets with rights to the Catellus name to TPG Capital , in 2011, for $505 million. [10] In 2006, ProLogis became a Fortune 1000 company and the ProLogis European Properties Fund became a public company . [6] Following aggressive expansion [9] and heavy borrowing, [2] ProLogis'' CEO Jeffrey Schwartz was replaced by Walter Rakowich in 2008, [11] who implemented cost-cutting efforts, raising capital and selling assets. [9] In December 2008, ProLogis sold a portfolio of assets to the Government of Singapore Investment Corporation , [9] with which it formed Global Logistic Properties . [12] The company sold its China operation', '["clm_wiki_prologis_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
