VALUES
  ('cs_axos_operations', 'org_axos', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Lending practices The bank has made larger loans to wealthy individuals that other banks have turned down, albeit at higher interest rates . [1] The company made a total of $400 million in loans to Donald Trump and is one of his largest lenders. [2] In March 2022, Axos funded a $100 million mortgage loan secured by Trump Tower . [3] [4] The company also financed Trump National Doral Miami . [2] As of June 30, 2025, approximately 28.7% of the company''s loans were secured by properties in Southern California , 7.1% were secured by properties in Northern California , 28.2% were secured by properties in New York , 12.0% were secured by properties in Florida , and 5.5% were secured by properties in Texas . [5] ^ body:not( ):not( ) Eavis, Peter (August 22, 2015). "An Internet Mortgage Provider Reaps the Rewards of Lending Boldly" . The New York Times . ^ a b Makortoff, Kalyeena (January 13, 2025). "Axos Financial: how the US bank became one of Trump''s biggest backers" . The Guardian . ^ McKnight, Patricia (March 9, 2022). "Virtual Bank Loans Trump $100M After Firm Warned of ''Unreliable'' Finances" . Newsweek . ^ Everson, Zach (March 11, 2022). "Checks &amp; Imbalances: Trump''s $100 Million Loan, Fortenberry Heads To Court" . Forbes . ^ Cite error: The named reference 10K was invoked but never defined (see the help page ).', '["clm_wiki_axos_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
