VALUES
  ('cs_pentair_history', 'org_pentair', 'overview', 'History',
   'Irish tax-resident US water treatment company Pentair plc American headquarters in St. Louis Park, Minnesota Type Public Traded as NYSE : PNR S&amp;P 500 component ISIN IE00BLS09M33 Industry Water &amp; Fluid Solutions Valves &amp; Controls Technical Solutions Water Treatment Solutions Founded July 6, 1966 ( 1966-07-06 ) in Arden Hills, Minnesota , US [1] [2] Headquarters London , UK Incorporated in Ireland Key people John L. Stauch ( President and CEO ) Bob Fishman ( CFO ) [3] Revenue US$ 4.08 billion (2024) Operating income US$804 million (2024) Net income US$625 million (2024) Total assets US$6.45 billion (2024) Total equity US$3.56 billion (2024) Number of employees 9,750 (2024) Website pentair Footnotes [4] Pentair plc ( PNR ) is an American water treatment company incorporated in Ireland with tax residency in UK , with its main U.S. office in Golden Valley , Minnesota . [5] Pentair was founded in the US, with 65% of company''s revenue coming from the US and Canada as of 2017. PNR was reorganized in 2014, shifting the corporate domicile from Switzerland to Ireland. [6] On April 30, 2018, PNR announced that it had completed the separation of its Water and Electrical businesses. [7] Now the company''s primary focus is on residential, commercial, industrial, municipal and infrastructure and agriculture applications. Its fiscal year 2021 revenues were US$3.8 billion and it employs approximately 11,250 people worldwide. [8] [9] ^ Pentair company profile at Funding Universe ^ Company Profile at Reuters ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Pentair PLC" . Reuters . Retrieved 17 August 2020 . ^ "2024 Annual Report (Form 10-K)" . US Securities and Exchange Commission. 25 February 2025. ^ "Pentair 2014 10-K Filing" . p. 1 . Retrieved 23 December 2015 . ^ "Pentair 2015 Investor Day Presentation" . p. 12 . Retrieved 23 December 2015 . ^ "Pentair Official website" . p. 1 . Retrieved 13 September 2018 . ^ "Pentair Official website" . Retrieved 5 August 2022 . ^ Cite error: The named reference pentaircom was invoked but never defined (see the help page ).', '["clm_wiki_pentair_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   20, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_pentair_leadership', 'org_pentair', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   '1960s Pentair was founded in August 1966 as Pentair Industries, Inc., with the name derived from the Greek root word "penta-" (alluding to the company''s five founders) and "air" (alluding to the company''s original product), and went public several months later. [1] The 5 founders were all formerly employed by Litton Industries. [2] Originally, they intended to manufacture high altitude research balloons, but they found sales to be less than expected. They acquired a local vacuum-forming business shortly thereafter and set about making canoes. This venture also met with less than expected sales. By 1968 the company was nearly bankrupt. Of the five original founders, one had died while three more had pulled out. The only remaining founder, Murray Harpole, brought in an outside investor, Ben Westby and they set about purchasing Peavey Paper Mills, Inc. The company was deeply in debt and badly managed, but the sale price was very inexpensive and Harpole believed that proper management would turn the company around in short order. ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Pentair Company History" . p. 1. Archived from the original on 24 December 2015 . Retrieved 23 December 2015 . ^ "Pentair, Inc. History" . Retrieved 28 February 2021 .', '["clm_wiki_pentair_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_pentair_operations', 'org_pentair', 'overview', 'Operations',
   '2020s In July 2022, Pentair announced that it had acquired Manitowoc Ice for $1.6 billion. [1] In April 2026, Pentair announced a leadership transition for its pool segment. Jerome Pedretti departed as Executive Vice President and Chief Executive Officer of Pentair Pool after more than 20 years with the company. Pentair also eliminated the standalone Chief Executive Officer, pool position and consolidated leadership of its Pool, Flow, and Water Solutions businesses under Executive Vice President De''Mon Wiggins. [2] [3] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Pentair Completes Transaction to Acquire Manitowoc Ice" . Pentair . Retrieved 2 March 2024 . ^ "Current Report (Form 8-K)" . SEC via Stock Titan . Retrieved 17 July 2026 . ^ "Pentair Announces Leadership Transition for Pentair Pool" . Pool Magazine . Retrieved 17 July 2026 .', '["clm_wiki_pentair_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 622. Public Storage (slug=public-storage, wiki='Public Storage')
