VALUES
  ('cs_planet-labs_leadership', 'org_planet-labs', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Flock satellite constellations Planet Labs mission control Planet''s PlanetScope Dove satellite constellation is designed to observe Earth. By using several small satellites, CubeSats, the constellation produces high resolution images of Earth — 3–5 metres (10–16 ft) per pixel. The flock collects images from latitudes that are within 52 degrees of Earth''s equator. [1] A large portion of the world''s agricultural regions and population lie within the area imaged by the flock. [1] Initially, the mission used the ISS ( International Space Station ) and different track launch vehicles to get in orbit. [2] Dove Further information: Flock-1 Planet''s Dove satellites are CubeSats that weigh 4 kilograms (8.8 lb) (1000 times lower than other commercial imaging satellites), 10 by 10 by 30 centimetres (3.9 in × 3.9 in × 11.8 in) in length, width and height, [3] orbit at a height of about 400 kilometres (250 mi) and provide imagery with a resolution of 3–5 metres (9.8–16.4 ft) and envisaged environmental, humanitarian, and business applications. [4] [5] Flock 1c , consisting of 11 Dove satellites, was successfully launched on 19 June 2014 with a Dnepr rocket on a record-breaking launch that carried to orbit the largest number of satellites up to that time, 37. [6] Flock 1b , consisting of 28 Dove satellites, was successfully launched to the ISS on 13 July 2014 with the Cygnus Orb-2 cargo mission. [7] All of those satellites have been deployed from the ISS but 6, that have been returned to Earth with the SpaceX CRS-5 mission 212 days later. [8] Flock 1d , consisting of 26 Dove satellites, was supposed to be carried to the ISS with the Cygnus Orb-3 cargo mission but was lost due the Antares rocket exploding seconds after liftoff. [9] Flock 1d'' , consisting of 2 Dove satellites, was launched successfully as a replacement of the lost Flock 1d group on 10 January 2015 with the SpaceX CRS-5 cargo mission [10] and was later deployed from the ISS on 3 March 2015. [8] Decayed on 13 October', '["clm_wiki_planet-labs_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
