  ('cs_freescale-semiconductor_leadership', 'org_freescale-semiconductor', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Divestiture from Motorola and first IPO As of 2003, Motorola Semiconductor Products Sector earned US$5.0 billion in semiconductor sales in 2002 (out of US$27 billion sales for all of Motorola). [1] [2] Motorola announced that their semiconductor division would be divested on October 6, 2003 and would have a temporary name SPS Spinco . [3] Freescale completed its Initial public offering (IPO) on July 16, 2004, at a price of US$13. In its announcement, it estimated the stock price to be US$17.50- 19.50 but following a cooling of the market towards tech stocks, it lowered its price to US$13. Existing shareholders of Motorola stock received 0.110415 shares of Freescale stock for every share of Motorola stock as a dividend which was distributed on December 2, 2004. [4] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Integrated Security Added to Motorola''s High-performance PowerQUICC™ III Processor Family" " (Press release). Motorola. 2003. Archived from the original on July 19, 2006. ^ "Motorola Semiconductor and Symbian launch 2.5G and 3G reference designs" (Press release). Symbian . 2002. &#91; permanent dead link &#93; ^ Charny, Ben (December 17, 2003). "Motorola chip unit files $2 billion IPO" . CNET . ^ "Motorola Shareholder Letter" (PDF) .', '["clm_wiki_freescale-semiconductor_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
