  ('cs_honeywell-aerospace_leadership', 'org_honeywell-aerospace', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Business Honeywell Aerospace Technologies is responsible for over thirty percent of Honeywell ''s total revenue with half from commercial contracts and half from defense contracts. The division generates $15 billion in annual sales. [1] In 2010, 75 percent of revenue was from the Americas. [2] Honeywell expects future revenue sources to be more geographically diverse as aviation grows in emerging markets like India and Africa . In these regions, a lack of ground support encourages the use of additional cockpit avionics to prevent crashes and determine flight routes. [3] Honeywell publishes a regular business aviation forecast, which acknowledged a significant decline from 2008 to 2010, but expected recovery to 2008 levels by 2017. [4] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Honeywell Announces Intent to Separate Automation and Aerospace, Enabling the Creation of Three Industry-leading Companies" . Honeywell. February 6, 2025. ^ "Investor Fact Sheet" . Honeyell. 2011 . Retrieved March 6, 2012 . &#91; permanent dead link &#93; ^ Fitzsimons, Bernard (November 13, 2011). "Honeywell Looks East While Innovating For Safe Growth" . Aviation International News . Retrieved December 27, 2011 . ^ Feliz, Emily (January 1, 2010). "Business Climb-Out" . Avionics Today . Retrieved December 27, 2011 .', '["clm_wiki_honeywell-aerospace_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
