VALUES
  ('cs_huntington-ingalls_operations', 'org_huntington-ingalls', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Ingalls Shipbuilding In 1938, Ingalls Shipbuilding Corporation was founded by Robert Ingersoll Ingalls Sr. (1882–1951), on the East Bank of the Pascagoula River in Mississippi. It started out building commercial ships until the 1950s, when Ingalls started bidding on Navy work. [1] Employing more than 11,000 employees, HII''s Ingalls Shipbuilding is the largest manufacturing employer in Mississippi and a major contributor to the economic growth of Alabama. For 85 years, Ingalls has designed, built and maintained amphibious ships, destroyers, and cutters for the U.S. Navy and the U.S. Coast Guard. The largest supplier of U.S. Navy surface combatants, Ingalls is simultaneously building four classes of ships. [2] Brian Blanchette, who served as vice president of quality at Ingalls from 2021 to 2024, began serving as president of Ingalls Shipbuilding and vice president of HII on January 1, 2025, [3] after his predecessor Kari Wilkinson was named president of Newport News Shipbuilding in November 2024. As president, Blanchette is responsible for all programs and operations at Ingalls, including the U.S. Navy’s amphibious assault and surface combatant ship programs. [4] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "OUR HISTORY" . HII . Retrieved 4 January 2024 . ^ "INGALLS SHIPBUILDING" . HII . Retrieved 4 January 2024 . ^ Staff (6 November 2024). "New Ingalls Shipbuilding president elected; current president taking new role" . WLOX . Retrieved 18 February 2026 . ^ "Kari Wilkinson" . HII . Retrieved 4 January 2024 .', '["clm_wiki_huntington-ingalls_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
