VALUES
  ('cs_keurig-dr-pepper_leadership', 'org_keurig-dr-pepper', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Background Main articles: Green Mountain Coffee Roasters and Dr Pepper Snapple Group Green Mountain Coffee Roasters, founded in 1979 in Vermont as a specialty coffee roaster, expanded nationwide in the late 20th and early 21st centuries. [1] [2] [3] It acquired Keurig, Inc. in 2006 and changed its name to Keurig Green Mountain in 2014. [4] [5] Dr Pepper Snapple Group was established in 2008 after its separation from Cadbury Schweppes, taking ownership of brands including Dr Pepper, Snapple, and 7 Up in the United States. [6] Both companies operated independently until 2018. ^ body:not( ):not( ) Goska, David (January 20, 1980). "Fragrance Fills the Air in Waitsfield" . The Burlington Free Press . p. 34 . Retrieved May 5, 2026 . ^ Ingram, Frederick C. (2000). Grant, Tina (ed.). International Directory of Company Histories . Vol. 31. Detroit, Michigan: St. James Press. pp. 227– 230. Archived from the original on May 14, 2016. ^ Carpenter, Murray (March 13, 2014). Caffeinated: How Our Daily Habit Helps, Hurts, and Hooks Us . Penguin. pp. 47– 56. ISBN 978-0-698-14850-5 . ^ "Green Mountain Coffee''s purchase of Keurig Inc. completed" . Boston Business Journal . June 16, 2006. Archived from the original on January 23, 2013 . Retrieved May 5, 2026 . ^ "New Green Mountain name shows Keurig connection" . USA TODAY . March 10, 2014. Archived from the original on December 25, 2024 . Retrieved May 5, 2026 . ^ Carolan, Michael (March 11, 2008). "Cadbury Schweppes to Complete Demerger of U.S. Unit on May 7" . Wall Street Journal . ISSN 0099-9660 . Retrieved May 5, 2026 .', '["clm_wiki_keurig-dr-pepper_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_keurig-dr-pepper_operations', 'org_keurig-dr-pepper', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Acquisition of JDE Peet''s and de-merger (2025-present) In August 2025, KDP announced that it was acquiring JDE Peet''s for €15.7 billion (≈$18 billion) and that it would combine Peet''s with its existing coffee division and spin them into a new corporate entity. As part of the plan, the company stated it intends to separate into two U.S.-listed companies, one focused on coffee and one on refreshment beverages, following completion of the acquisition. [1] [2] In October 2025, KDP struck a deal with private-equity companies KKR and Apollo Global Management , along with Goldman Sachs to secure $7 billion in funding to support the acquisition and the planned separation of its coffee and beverage businesses into two public companies. [3] In January 2026, KDP formally launched its all-cash takeover offer for JDE Peet’s at €31.85 per share after obtaining competition clearances. JDE Peet’s board supported the offer, and shareholders holding 69% of the company’s shares had committed to accept it. [4] The following month, the company secured an additional $1.5 billion in equity funding for the deal. KDP also announced that it was targeting the separation of the combined business into Global Coffee Co and Beverage Co by the end of 2026. [5] In April 2026, as part of its planned post-acquisition separation, Keurig Dr Pepper named JDE Peet’s chief executive Rafael Oliveira to lead its coffee operating unit and the future Global Coffee Co, while Tim Cofer was set to lead Beverage Co. [6] ^ body:not( ):not( ) Hirsch, Lauren; Creswell, Julie (August 25, 2025). "Keurig Dr Pepper to Acquire Peet''s in $18 Billion Deal" . The New York Times . Retrieved August 25, 2025 . ^ Rabiega, Mateusz; Tabassum, Juveria (August 25, 2025). "Dr Pepper takes a shot at Nestlé with $18 bln takeover of Dutch coffee giant" . Reuters . Retrieved December 4, 2025 . ^ Cooper, Laura (October 27, 2025). "Keurig Dr Pepper Turns to Private Capital to Back $18 Billion Deal" . The Wall Street Journal . Retrieved Oc', '["clm_wiki_keurig-dr-pepper_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
