VALUES
  ('cs_nextera-energy_history', 'org_nextera-energy', 'overview', 'History',
   'American utility company html body Parts of this article (those related to Avangrid transmission line project) need to be updated . Please help update this article to reflect recent events or newly available information. ( April 2026 ) NextEra Energy, Inc. Formerly FPL Group (1984–2010) Type Public Traded as NYSE : NEE DJUA component S&amp;P 100 component S&amp;P 500 component Industry Electric power industry Energy development Renewable energy Founded 1984 &#59;&#32;42 years ago ( 1984 ) as FPL Group Headquarters Juno Beach, Florida , U.S. Area served United States and Canada Key people John W. Ketchum ( Chairman , President and CEO of NEE) [1] Scott Bores( President and CEO of FPL) Brian Bolster ( President and CEO of NEER) Services Electricity generation electric power transmission electric power distribution Revenue US$ 24.8 billion (2024) Operating income US$7.48 billion (2024) Net income US$6.0730 billion (2024) Total assets US$190.1 billion (2024) Total equity US$60.5 billion (2024) Number of employees 16,700 (2024) Subsidiaries Florida Power &amp; Light NextEra Energy Resources Website nexteraenergy Footnotes [2] [3] [4] NextEra Energy, Inc. is an American energy company that is the world''s largest electric utility holding company by market capitalization, with a valuation of over $187 billion as of July 2026. [5] NextEra Energy had revenues of $24.8 billion and 16,700 employees throughout the US and Canada in 2024. [6] [7] It has a current generating capacity of 73 gigawatts . [8] Its subsidiaries include Florida Power &amp; Light (FPL), NextEra Energy Resources (NEER), XPLR Energy Partners , and NextEra Energy Services. FPL, the largest of its subsidiaries, is the third largest electric utility company in the United States. [9] NEER, together with its affiliated entities, is the world''s largest generator of renewable energy from wind and solar. [10] In addition to wind and solar, NextEra Energy Resources owns and operates generating plants powered by natural gas, nuclear energy, and oil. [9] As of March 2025, approximately 36% of NextEra Energy''s generating capacity was from fossil fuels and non-renewables, down from 41% in 2020. [7] [8] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "A Commitment to Innovation and Excellence | NextEra Energy Leadership" . NextEra Energy . Retrieved July 16, 2025 . ^ "SEC Form 10-K" . U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission . February 14, 2025 . Retrieved February 18, 2025 . ^ "Board of Directors" . NextEra Energy . Retrieved October 20, ', '["clm_wiki_nextera-energy_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
