VALUES
  ('cs_bok-financial_leadership', 'org_bok-financial', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Footprint and family of brands Beginning in 1994, the company steadily expanded its footprint beyond Oklahoma, with moves into: Arkansas in 1994 through the acquisition of a bank holding company with operations in northwest Arkansas. [1] Texas through the acquisition of two banks that were merged and renamed Bank of Texas in 1997. [2] [3] New Mexico in 1998 with the acquisition of 17 branches [1] from BankAmericaCorp that were renamed Bank of Albuquerque. [3] Colorado in 2003 with the formation of Colorado State Bank [1] and the acquisition in October 2018 of Colorado-based CoBiz Financial, previously Colorado Business Bank. [4] The Colorado and Arizona operations were rebranded as "BOK Financial" in 2018. [5] Arizona in 2005 with the formation of Bank of Arizona. [1] Missouri in 2016 through the acquisition of MBT Bancshares, the parent company of Missouri Bank and Trust of Kansas City. [6] In October, 2019 the company rebranded its operations in Arkansas, Kansas, and Missouri as BOK Financial. [7] [8] Tulsa''s BOK Center, designed by noted architect César Pelli . ^ a b c d Cite error: The named reference 100years was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ body:not( ):not( ) "BOK Financial Acquires Two Dallas Companies" . The Oklahoman . March 2, 1999. ^ a b Boyd, Danny M. "BOK Financial Corp Announces Acquisition" . The Oklahoman . Retrieved March 7, 2023 . ^ " ''A Bank For All People'': Remembering The Women''s Bank" . Denver Public Library History . March 22, 2022 . Retrieved March 7, 2023 . ^ "BOK Financial Corporation to Acquire CoBiz Financial" (Press release). GlobeNewswire . June 18, 2018. ^ "BOK Financial Receives Regulatory Approvals to Acquire MBT Bancshares" (Press release). GlobeNewswire . November 16, 2016. ^ Corporation, BOK Financial (October 28, 2019). "Bank of Arkansas to Rebrand to BOK Financial" . GlobeNewswire News Room . Retrieved March 7, 2023 . ^ Corporation, BOK Financial (October 28, 2019). "Mobank to Rebrand to BOK Financial" . Glo', '["clm_wiki_bok-financial_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
