VALUES
  ('cs_six-flags_leadership', 'org_six-flags', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Cedar Fair Main article: Cedar Fair Cedar Fair, L.P was founded in 1983 after Cedar Point , an amusement park in Sandusky, Ohio , acquired Valleyfair in Shakopee, Minnesota , in 1978. The company''s name was derived from both parks—"Cedar" from Cedar Point and "Fair" from Valleyfair. Cedar Fair became publicly traded on April 29, 1987. [1] [2] The company would increase its portfolio through acquisitions of amusement parks throughout the United States. Through its 1997 acquisition of Knott''s Berry Farm , the company gained licensing rights to use Peanuts intellectual properties within its parks. [3] In 2006, Cedar Fair acquired five amusement parks in the purchase of Paramount Parks from CBS Corporation for US$1.24 billion. [4] Shortly after the acquisitions, the company would begin to do business as under the name Cedar Fair Entertainment Company. [5] Cedar Fair faced multiple failed acquisition attempts prior to the merger. Private equity company Apollo Global Management announced plans to take over Cedar Fair in late-2009 and make it into a private company . However, Cedar Fair''s unitholders did not support the acquisition, which derailed the transaction in 2010. [6] In 2019, Six Flags approached Cedar Fair with a cash-and-stock acquisition offer, which was quickly rejected. [7] [8] Almost three years later in 2022, SeaWorld Entertainment (now United Parks &amp; Resorts) made an unsolicited all-cash bid to buy Cedar Fair for $3.4 billion; this offer was rejected two weeks later. [9] [10] In order to lower overall corporate debts, the company sold California''s Great America in Santa Clara, California , on June 27, 2022, to real estate developer Prologis for $310 million. [11] By the end of 2022, Cedar Fair reported it had around $2.2 billion in net debt . [12] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Cedar Fair, Form 10-K, Annual Report, Filing Date Mar 23, 1994" . Securities and Exchange Commission . Archived from the original on March 4, 2016 . Retrieved March 21, 2013 . ^ "Cedar F', '["clm_wiki_six-flags_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_six-flags_operations', 'org_six-flags', 'overview', 'Operations',
   '2024: Immediate post-merger integration The company''s headquarters were relocated five miles northeast of its Carowinds park (pictured) in Charlotte, North Carolina . After the merger''s completion on July 1, 2024, the president and CEO of Cedar Fair, Richard Zimmerman, became president and CEO of the new combined company. Selim Bassoul, the president and CEO of the former Six Flags company, became the executive chairman of the company''s board of directors. [1] The combined company''s headquarters was relocated to Charlotte, North Carolina , five miles northeast of its Carowinds park, with significant administrative and financial operations based at Cedar Fair''s former headquarters in Sandusky, Ohio . [2] [3] Cedar Fair had held significant administrative offices in Charlotte, North Carolina, prior to the merger. [4] Six Flags clarified at the time of merger that the company did not anticipate making any major changes at the park-level and that all properties would retain their pre-merger names. [5] Stocks for both Cedar Fair and the former Six Flags company ceased trading on the New York Stock Exchange, and the newly formed entity commenced trading on July 2, 2024 under the ticker symbol FUN. [6] In late-2024, Six Flags outlined plans for a portfolio optimization in the company, which may include closing or selling off some of its locations. [7] The company filed plans with the United States Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) on December 17, 2024, that it would acquire the remaining limited partnership units of Six Flags Over Georgia , Hurricane Harbor Atlanta , and Six Flags White Water by January 12, 2027. The transaction would cost US$332.6 million, and would increase Six Flags'' ownership of the three parks from 31.5% to full ownership. [8] ^ Cite error: The named reference Merger-Glaser was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ Cite error: The named reference Merger-PR was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ body:not( ):not( ) Mucc', '["clm_wiki_six-flags_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
