VALUES
  ('cs_barnes-noble_leadership', 'org_barnes-noble', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   '19th century: foundations Clifford Noble in 1893 The company''s official history shows the company being founded in 1873, when Charles M. Barnes founded a book business in Wheaton, Illinois. In 1917, Barnes''s son, William, joined G. Clifford Noble in establishing Barnes &amp; Noble. [1] A major part of what would become Barnes &amp; Noble was founded in 1886 as a bookstore called Arthur Hinds &amp; Company , [2] located at 4 Cooper Institute in the Cooper Union Building in New York City. [3] [4] [5] In the fall of 1886, Gilbert Clifford Noble from Westfield, Massachusetts , who had graduated from Harvard College earlier that year, [6] was hired to work there as a clerk. [7] In 1894, Noble was made a partner, and the name of the shop was changed to Hinds &amp; Noble . [8] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Barnes &amp; Noble History" . Barnes &amp; Noble, Inc . Retrieved January 26, 2026 . ^ Hyken, Shep (April 8, 2018). "Barnes &amp; Noble, Amazon, Independents: Who''s Disrupting Whom?" . Forbes . Retrieved September 8, 2019 . ^ Xenophon . The First Four Books of Xenophon''s Anabasis . Translated by Watson, John Selby . New York City: Arthur Hinds &amp; Company . Retrieved June 28, 2021 &#8211; via gutenberg . 4 Cooper Institute, New York City ^ "Barnes &amp; Noble to Move", The Bookseller and Stationer , January 1. 1922 p. 13 ^ The Noble Legacy: The Story of Gilbert Clifford Noble, Cofounder of the Barnes &amp; Noble and Noble &amp; Noble Book Companies by Betty N. Turner. iUniverse: 2006 ISBN 0595374786 , 9780595374786, page 71 ^ "Harvard Mirador Viewer" . Iiif . ^ The Noble Legacy: The Story of Gilbert Clifford Noble, Cofounder of the Barnes &amp; Noble and Noble &amp; Noble Book Companies by Betty N. Turner. iUniverse: 2006 ISBN 0595374786 , 9780595374786, page 65 ^ The Noble Legacy: The Story of Gilbert Clifford Noble, Cofounder of the Barnes &amp; Noble and Noble &amp; Noble Book Companies by Betty N. Turner. iUniverse: 2006 ISBN 0595374786 , 9780595374786, page 101', '["clm_wiki_barnes-noble_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_barnes-noble_operations', 'org_barnes-noble', 'overview', 'Operations',
   '20th century: expansion 1900–1919 In 1901, Hinds &amp; Noble moved to 31–35 W. 15th Street. [1] In 1917, Noble bought out Hinds and entered into a partnership with William Barnes, son of his old friend Charles Barnes; the name of the store was changed to Barnes &amp; Noble soon after. [2] [3] Charles Barnes had previously founded an Illinois bookstore that would later evolve into the Follett Corporation after William Barnes left the company. [4] 1920–1939 In 1930, Noble sold his share of the company to William Barnes''s son, John Wilcox Barnes. [5] Noble died on June 6, 1936, at the age of 72. [6] In 1932, at the height of the Great Depression , the bookstore moved its flagship location to 18th Street and Fifth Avenue , [7] which served as the company''s flagship location until its closure in 2014. The Noble family retained ownership of an associated publishing business, and Barnes &amp; Noble opened a new publishing division in 1931. [5] 1940–1959 In 1940, the store was one of the first businesses to feature Muzak . It underwent a major renovation the following year. [8] That decade, the company opened stores in Brooklyn and Chicago. William Barnes died in 1945, at the age of 78, and his son John Wilcox Barnes assumed full control. [9] The company underwent a significant expansion between the 1950s and the 1960s, opening an additional retail store on 23rd Street in Manhattan, as well as shops near the City University of New York , Harvard , and other Northeast college campuses . [10] 1960–1979 Barnes &amp; Noble old corporate headquarters, on Fifth Avenue in the Flatiron District neighborhood of Manhattan, New York City 5th Avenue store sign John Barnes died in 1964, and the company was sold to the conglomerate Amtel two years later. [11] The business was purchased in 1971 by Leonard Riggio , who has been credited as one of the founders, [12] [13] for $1.2 million. [10] By then, it consisted only of "a significantly reduced wholesale operation and a single retail loc', '["clm_wiki_barnes-noble_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
