VALUES
  ('cs_solarcity_overview', 'org_solarcity', 'overview', 'Overview',
   'SolarCity Corporation was a publicly traded company headquartered in Fremont, California, that sold and installed solar energy generation systems as well as other related products and services to residential, commercial, and industrial customers. The company was founded on July 4, 2006, by Peter and Lyndon Rive, the cousins of SpaceX and Tesla CEO Elon Musk. Tesla acquired SolarCity in 2016, at a cost of approximately $2.6 billion and reorganized its solar business into Tesla Energy.', '["clm_wiki_solarcity_overview"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   10, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_solarcity_history', 'org_solarcity', 'overview', 'History',
   'American solar energy company Not to be confused with Solar Cities in Australia . SolarCity Corporation Original SolarCity headquarters in Foster City, California Type Public Traded as Nasdaq : SCTY (2012–2016) Industry Solar energy generation Founded July 4, 2006 &#59;&#32;20 years ago ( 2006-07-04 ) Founders Lyndon and Peter Rive Defunct November 21, 2016 ( 2016-11-21 ) Fate Acquired by Tesla, Inc. Successor Tesla Energy Headquarters Fremont, California , United States Key people Lyndon Rive ( CEO ) Peter Rive ( CTO ) Elon Musk ( Chairman ) Website solarcity (defunct) Footnotes [1] body body This article is part of a series about Elon Musk Personal Awards and honors Business career Views Family Musk Foundation International relations Legal affairs Public image Texas Institute of Technology and Science Wealth Companies Zip2 PayPal X SpaceX Starlink Starshield xAI X Corp. Twitter under Elon Musk acquisition SEC investigation Tesla, Inc. SolarCity OpenAI Neuralink The Boring Company Terafab Thud Politics Activities America PAC America Party Building America''s Future Dept. of Govt. Efficiency Salute controversy Trade unions Tesla Protests Tesla Takedown vandalism Trump feud In the arts and media Character Limit Elon Musk by Vance Elon Musk''s Crash Course Elon Musk by Isaacson Filmography Ludicrous Make America Wait Again " The Musk Who Fell to Earth " Musk (film) Power Play Return to Space Revenge of the Electric Car The Space Barons html v t e SolarCity Corporation was a publicly traded company headquartered in Fremont, California , that sold and installed solar energy generation systems as well as other related products and services to residential, commercial, and industrial customers. The company was founded on July 4, 2006, by Peter and Lyndon Rive , the cousins of SpaceX and Tesla CEO Elon Musk . Tesla acquired SolarCity in 2016, at a cost of approximately $2.6 billion (equivalent to $3.5 billion in 2025) and reorganized its solar business into Tesla Energy . SolarCity heavily focused on door-to-door sales of leased systems, where customers would pay no upfront costs, but agreed to purchase the power generated by those panels from the company for 20 years. The business model became the most popular in the US and made the company the largest residential solar installer, but it caused SolarCity to have over $1.5 billion in debt by the time of its acquisition in 2016 (equivalent to $2 billion in 2025). [2] Prior to its acquisition by Tesla, the two compani', '["clm_wiki_solarcity_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   20, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_solarcity_leadership', 'org_solarcity', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Workforce reduction SolarCity installation vehicles During 2015, the number of employees had grown by 69%; [1] at the end of 2015, SolarCity had 15,273 employees. To preserve cash, [2] SolarCity eliminated 20% of its total workforce in 2016, [3] ending 2016 with 12,243 employees. [3] This was the first time in the company''s history that it cut its workforce. [4] The job cuts affected workers across the entire company: [3] [1] 22% of jobs were cut in operations, installations, and manufacturing; and 27% in sales and marketing. In August 2016, the company announced that it planned to take up to $5 million in charges to cover its planned layoffs. The company also cut the salaries of its two co-founders from $275,000 to $1 per year. [5] ^ a b body:not( ):not( ) "Tesla''s SolarCity Cut 20% of Its Staff Last Year" . Fortune . March 2, 2017. Archived from the original on March 14, 2021 . Retrieved March 2, 2017 . ^ Rosen, Ben (March 2, 2017). "SolarCity''s workforce shrinks – but predictions for solar point up" . Christian Science Monitor . ISSN 0882-7729 . Archived from the original on March 2, 2017 . Retrieved March 2, 2017 . ^ a b c Robinson, David (March 2, 2017). "SolarCity, gearing up to begin work here, cut 3,000 jobs in 2016" . The Buffalo News . Archived from the original on November 14, 2019 . Retrieved March 2, 2017 . ^ "Why Tesla Had to Lay Off 3,000 SolarCity Employees" . Madison . March 2, 2017. Archived from the original on May 27, 2021 . Retrieved March 2, 2017 . ^ Akhtar, Allana (August 19, 2016). "SolarCity announces layoffs, cuts executive pay to $1" . wgrz . WGRZ . Retrieved July 6, 2024 .', '["clm_wiki_solarcity_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_solarcity_operations', 'org_solarcity', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Acquisition by Tesla, Inc. On August 1, 2016, Tesla announced that it would be acquiring the company in an all-stock $2.6 billion transaction as Tesla''s mission since its inception has been "to accelerate the world''s transition to sustainable energy ." [1] As part of Elon Musk''s "The Secret Tesla Motors Master Plan," Tesla sought to expedite the world''s move from a mine-and-burn hydrocarbon economy towards a solar electric economy. [2] The proposal for acquisition was approved by antitrust regulators. [3] [4] More than 85% of unaffiliated shareholders (affiliated shareholders are those who hold executive positions at either company [5] ) from Tesla and SolarCity voted to approve the acquisition on November 17, 2016, [6] [7] allowing the acquisition to close on November 21, 2016. Some investors criticized the deal, calling it "a misguided effort to rescue two companies that depend on investors and the government for operating cash." [8] In April 2022, a Delaware court ruled in favor of defendant Elon Musk in a shareholder lawsuit over Tesla''s $2.6 billion acquisition of SolarCity. “[The] Tesla Board meaningfully vetted the Acquisition, and Elon did not stand in its way,” read the opinion by Vice Chancellor Joseph Slights. “Equally if not more important, the preponderance of the evidence reveals that Tesla paid a fair price—SolarCity was, at a minimum, worth what Tesla paid for it,” Slights added. [9] [10] The court also noted that since the acquisition "Tesla’s value has massively increased". [11] In April 2017, the chief policy officer of SolarCity, John Wellinghoff, left SolarCity. [12] In June 2017, Lyndon Rive left SolarCity, [13] and Peter Rive left shortly thereafter. [14] By 2019, Tesla''s solar panel market share was falling, prompting the company to cut its sales force. Revenue from Tesla''s energy generation and storage operations from January to September 2019 fell 7% from a year earlier to $1.1 billion. [15] ^ body:not( ):not( ) Desjardins, Jeff (April 28, ', '["clm_wiki_solarcity_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
