VALUES
  ('cs_sunpower_leadership', 'org_sunpower', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Early history SunPower was founded on April 24, 1985, by Richard Swanson , [1] who was a Stanford University professor focused on electrical engineering. [2] Swanson studied solar power efficiency in the Stanford Electronics Laboratory with funding from research grants. [3] &#58;&#8202;468&#8202; After breaking a record for solar power efficiency in lab conditions, he took a sabbatical to start SunPower and commercialize the technology. [2] [4] Initially, the company was called Eos and was funded with $2,000 in savings between Swanson and his friend Richard Crane. [3] &#58;&#8202;468&#8202; In 1989, Robert Lorenzini invested in the company, became its chairman, and changed the name to SunPower. [3] &#58;&#8202;468&#8202; Some of SunPower''s early revenues were from research grants [1] and using its manufacturing facilities to create silicon wafers for semiconductor companies. [3] &#58;&#8202;468&#8202; Interest grew as SunPower completed prototype installations [2] and portable electronics that use solar power became more popular. [3] &#58;&#8202;468&#8202; Swanson resigned from his academic position at Stanford in 1991, in order to focus on SunPower full-time. [4] The company''s revenues grew from $600,000 in 1989 to $1.4 million in 1995, and $6 million in 1996. [3] &#58;&#8202;468&#8202; However, by 2001 the company was anticipating having to lay off half of its employees. [5] ^ a b body:not( ):not( ) Hull, Dana (April 23, 2010). "2010: Q&amp;A with Dick Swanson, founder and president of SunPower" . The Mercury News . Archived from the original on September 10, 2021 . Retrieved May 31, 2021 . He founded SunPower on April 24, 1985, and the small company lived on research grants and small projects until... ^ a b c Chandler, David (May 7, 1990). "Renewable Energy Some Say Alternative Fuels Could Supply 80 Percent of the Nation''s Needs by 2010". The Boston Globe . p. 27. Richard Swanson, a professor of electrical engineering at Stanford University, created cells that ho', '["clm_wiki_sunpower_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_sunpower_operations', 'org_sunpower', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Growth SunPower founder Richard Swanson''s former classmate, T.J. Rodgers , was the CEO of Cypress Semiconductor and took an interest in investing in the company. [1] At first, the Cypress board wasn''t willing to invest, so Rodgers invested $750,000 of his own money. [2] Starting with an investment of $8 million, Cypress eventually invested about $150 million, [2] acquiring a controlling interest in SunPower in 2002. [1] Cypress appointed Tom Werner as the new CEO the following year. [3] &#58;&#8202;469&#8202; Demand for SunPower''s products increased in the early 2000s, due to rising utility costs, government subsidies, and its new A-300 solar cell. [3] &#58;&#8202;469&#8202; [4] In particular, SunPower grew in Germany and California, where new government subsidies were being introduced. [4] By 2005, SunPower was not yet profitable, [3] &#58;&#8202;469&#8202; but had $200 million in backlogged orders. [4] Revenues increased from $5 million in 2003 to $78.7 million in 2005. [3] &#58;&#8202;469&#8202; As the company was getting closer to profitability, it filed an initial public offering . [3] &#58;&#8202;469&#8202; The 2005 offering raised $138.6 million in funding. [1] The following year, SunPower was profitable for the first time with $236.5 million in revenues. [3] &#58;&#8202;469&#8202; SunPower moved into a larger corporate headquarters location in San Jose, California and secured several contracts with major retailers for solar panel installations. [3] &#58;&#8202;469–70&#8202; In 2007, SunPower announced plans to expand its manufacturing facility five-fold [5] and build a second factory. [6] SunPower collaborated with PowerLight to develop its roofing-tile solar product called SunTile. [3] &#58;&#8202;469&#8202; [7] In order to combine their R&amp;D efforts, SunPower acquired PowerLight for $265 million, in January 2007. [3] &#58;&#8202;469–70&#8202; Analysts estimated the acquisition doubled SunPower''s size. [7] Shortly afterwards, PowerLight secured a $330 mi', '["clm_wiki_sunpower_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 965. Teradata (slug=teradata, wiki='Teradata')
