VALUES
  ('cs_kansas-city-southern_leadership', 'org_kansas-city-southern', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Subsidiaries A Panama Canal Railway locomotive sits in a station at Colón, Panama In addition to KCS, KCSM and PCRC, Kansas City Southern’s subsidiaries include: Gateway Eastern Railway Company ( GWWE ) is a wholly owned subsidiary of KCS. GWWE provides rail service over about 17 miles (27 km) of track in the East St. Louis, Illinois , area. [1] Meridian Speedway, LLC (MSLLC) is a majority-owned, consolidated subsidiary that owns the former KCS rail line between Meridian, Mississippi and Shreveport, Louisiana, which is a portion of the KCS rail route between Dallas and Meridian, and is known as the " Meridian Speedway ." Norfolk Southern Corporation (NS), through its wholly owned subsidiary, the Alabama Great Southern Railroad , owns a minority interest in MSLLC. [1] Southern Capital, Development, and Industrial Services Companies: The Southern Capital company consists of bulk storage facilities complete with ocean terminals (Pabtex) along with a tie and timber plant (Trans-Serve, Inc., also known as Superior Tie and Timber). The Southern Development Company is a holding company that owns various properties. The Southern Capital Corporation, LLC is a leasing company owned jointly by KCS and GATX Capital Corporation of San Francisco, California , Southern Capital Corporation has the rail assets that once belonged to Carland and KCS, as well as the loan portfolio once owned by Southern Leasing Corporation. [1] The Texas Mexican Railway Company ( TexMex ) is wholly owned by KCS. TexMex consists of a 157-mile (253 km) railway that connects Corpus Christi, Texas to Laredo, Texas . TexMex also owns 400 miles (640 km) of Union Pacific trackage rights that spans from Beaumont, Texas to Robstown, Texas . With its trackage rights and physical railway, the Texas Mexican Railway connects KCSM and KCS at Laredo and Beaumont. TexMex also owns the Texas Mexican Railway International Bridge . It is the only rail bridge that connects the United States with Mexico through Laredo, and', '["clm_wiki_kansas-city-southern_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_kansas-city-southern_operations', 'org_kansas-city-southern', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Merger with Canadian Pacific Railway In March 2021, Calgary -based Canadian Pacific Railway offered over $25 billion to purchase KCS. The purchase would allow the Canadian company to create the first rail network connecting the United States, Mexico and Canada. [1] However, in May 2021, Canadian National Railway announced a superior bid to CP''s, which KCS management later agreed to support. [2] In August 2021, CP announced an increased bid that, while still less than CN''s bid, was claimed by CP to have a greater chance of regulatory approval. [3] While KCS''s board agreed to CN''s bid, shareholder and regulatory approvals were still required; on August 31, the US Surface Transportation Board (STB) denied a voting trust between CN and KCS. [4] With the decision by the STB, KCS re-engaged with CP on CP''s original offer. [5] [6] On September 15, KCS confirmed that it had terminated its agreement with CN and would support CP''s revised offer. [7] Kansas City Southern''s shareholders voted to approve the merger on December 10, 2021. [8] The transaction closed in early 2022, following which KCS was held in a previously approved voting trust pending approval by the STB; the combined company was to be named Canadian Pacific Kansas City (CPKC). [9] [10] The combined "Canadian Pacific Kansas City Limited" would form the first railroad serving all of the countries in the North American trade zone (Canada, Mexico and the United States). [10] On March 15, 2023, the STB approved the merger between the two companies, which was completed on April 14, 2023. [11] [12] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "CP Rail Said Near $25 Billion Kansas City Southern Deal" . Bloomberg . 2021-03-21 . Retrieved 2021-03-21 . ^ "CN to combine with Kansas City Southern" . BusinessWire . 2021-05-21. ^ Atkins, Eric (August 10, 2021). "Canadian Pacific hikes bid for Kansas City Southern to US$27.2-billion, renewing takeover battle with rival CN" . The Globe and Mail . Retrieved August 10, 2021 . ^ Stephens, Bill (31 August', '["clm_wiki_kansas-city-southern_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 973. Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (slug=federal-deposit-insurance, wiki='Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation')
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_federal-deposit-insurance_overview', 'org_federal-deposit-insurance', 'overview', 'Overview',
   'The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is a United States government corporation supplying deposit insurance to depositors in American commercial banks and savings banks. The FDIC was created by the Banking Act of 1933, enacted during the Great Depression to restore trust in the American banking system. More than one-third of banks failed in the years before the FDIC''s creation, and bank runs were common. The insurance limit was initially US$2,500 per ownership category, and this has been increased several times over the years. Since the enactment of the Dodd–Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act in 2010, the FDIC insures deposits in member banks up to $250,000 per ownership category. FDIC insurance is backed by the full faith and credit of the government of the United States, and according to the FDIC, "since its start in 1933 no depositor has ever lost a penny of FDIC-insured funds".', '["clm_wiki_federal-deposit-insurance_overview"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   10, 1, 1, unixepoch());
