VALUES
  ('cs_alexion-pharmaceuticals_history', 'org_alexion-pharmaceuticals', 'overview', 'History',
   'American healthcare company Alexion Pharmaceuticals, Inc. Offices in New Haven, Connecticut Type Subsidiary Traded as Nasdaq : ALXN Industry Pharmaceutical industry Founded 1992 &#59;&#32;34 years ago ( 1992 ) Founder Leonard Bell Headquarters Boston, Massachusetts , U.S. Key people Leonard Bell ( chairman ) Ludwig N. Hantson ( CEO ) Products Eculizumab (Soliris) Ravulizumab (Ultomiris) Asfotase alfa (Strensiq) Sebelipase alfa (Kanuma) Andexanet alfa (Andexxa) Revenue US$ 6.069 billion (2020) Net income US$603 million (2020) Total assets US$18.103 billion (2020) Total equity US$11.651 billion (2020) Number of employees 2,525 (2020) Parent AstraZeneca Website www Footnotes [1] Alexion Pharmaceuticals, Inc. , a subsidiary of AstraZeneca , is a pharmaceutical company headquartered in Boston, Massachusetts , specializing in orphan drugs for rare diseases . Its products include eculizumab (Soliris) and ravulizumab (Ultomiris), both used to treat the rare disorders of atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome (aHUS) and paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH); asfotase alfa (Strensiq), used to treat hypophosphatasia ; sebelipase alfa (Kanuma), used to treat lysosomal acid lipase deficiency , and andexanet alfa (Andexxa), used to stop life threatening or uncontrollable bleeding in people who are taking rivaroxaban or apixaban . [1] With costs that can reach as much as $2 million per year, the drugs manufactured by Alexion are some of the most expensive drugs worldwide. [2] ^ a b body:not( ):not( ) "Alexion Pharmaceuticals 2020 Form 10-K Annual Report" . U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission . ^ Thomas, Katie; Abelson, Reed (August 25, 2019). "The $6 Million Drug Claim" . The New York Times .', '["clm_wiki_alexion-pharmaceuticals_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   20, 1, 1, unixepoch());
