VALUES
  ('cs_zendesk_history', 'org_zendesk', 'overview', 'History',
   'American customer service software company Zendesk, Inc. Type Private Traded as NYSE : ZEN (2014–2022) Industry Software as a service Founded 2007 &#59;&#32;19 years ago ( 2007 ) in Copenhagen , Denmark Founders Mikkel Svane Alexander Aghassipour Morten Primdahl Headquarters 181 Fremont , San Francisco, California , U.S. Number of locations 19 (2022) Key people Tom Eggemeier ( CEO ) Revenue US$ 1.34 billion (2021) Operating income US$ −166.68 million (2021) Net income US$ −223.64 million (2021) Total assets US$ 2.45 billion (2021) Total equity US$ 489.22 million (2021) Owner Hellman &amp; Friedman (2022–present) Permira (2022–present) Number of employees 5,860 (2021) Website zendesk Footnotes [1] Zendesk, Inc. is an American company that provides software-as-a-service (SaaS) products related to customer support , sales, and other customer communications. The company was founded in Copenhagen, Denmark, in 2007 and is headquartered in San Francisco, California, United States. Zendesk raised about $86 million in venture capital investments before going public in 2014. [2] On November 22, 2022, Zendesk announced that it had been acquired by a group of investors led by Hellman &amp; Friedman and Permira for approximately $10.2 billion. [3] ^ body:not( ):not( ) Zendesk 10-K FY 2021 (PDF) , 2021-12-31 ^ Dealroom, .co (11 November 2022). "Zendesk company information, funding and investors" . app .co . Retrieved 11 November 2022 . ^ "Consortium led by Hellman &amp; Friedman and Permira completes acquisition of Zendesk" . Permira . Archived from the original on 2022-12-28 . Retrieved 2022-12-28 .', '["clm_wiki_zendesk_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   20, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_zendesk_leadership', 'org_zendesk', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Origins and funding Zendesk was founded in Copenhagen, Denmark in 2007 by three friends: Morten Primdahl, Alexander Aghassipour, and Mikkel Svane . [1] [2] The founders started developing the Zendesk software in Svane''s loft. [3] Initially, Zendesk was funded by the cofounders themselves, each doing consulting jobs to support their families. [3] [4] Within a few months of the Zendesk software-as-a-service product being released in the fall of 2007, it had about 1,000 trial customers. [3] Initially, interest in the software spread slowly through word-of-mouth [3] predominantly among other startups. [5] Adoption accelerated in 2008, due to an increased interest in responding to customer complaints on social media and after Twitter started using Zendesk. [3] Venture capital investors were willing to invest in Zendesk only if the company moved to the United States, [1] where most of their customers were. [3] In 2009 Zendesk moved to Boston, Massachusetts. About six months later, they made a second move, this time to San Francisco, California. [1] [6] The company raised $500,000 in seed funding, which was followed by a series A funding round for an undisclosed amount [7] and a series B round for $6 million. [3] Zendesk raised $19 million in a series C funding round in December 2010, [1] followed by $60 million in a series D funding round in 2012. [8] This brought total venture capital funding to about $86 million. [1] ^ a b c d e body:not( ):not( ) Shinal, John (July 28, 2011). "Startup Strives to Make Customer Service a More ''Zen'' Experience" . Entrepreneur . Retrieved October 2, 2020 . ^ Kim, Eugene (December 16, 2014). "How This CEO Was Able To Take His Company Public When Everybody Else Was Panicking" . Business Insider . Retrieved October 2, 2020 . ^ a b c d e f g Rao, Leena (November 16, 2013). "From Its Beginnings In A Denmark Loft, Zendesk''s Steady Rise To The Top Of The Helpdesk Heap" . TechCrunch . Retrieved August 16, 2023 . ^ Locke, Laura (July 30, 2014). "Th', '["clm_wiki_zendesk_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_zendesk_operations', 'org_zendesk', 'overview', 'Operations',
   '2010–2016 In 2010, many customers threatened to leave Zendesk in response to a substantial price increase after new features were introduced. [1] Zendesk apologized, abandoned the pricing, and promised never to change pricing on pre-existing customers for features they already have. [2] That same year, Zendesk created its first sales team. [3] Zendesk moved to larger offices in the Tenderloin neighborhood of San Francisco in 2011. [4] The city and county of San Francisco provided Zendesk with a six-year payroll tax incentive to move to the area in exchange for doing community service. [5] [4] Zendesk grew internationally, and offices were established in Dublin, Copenhagen, London, and Australia. [5] [6] In 2017, Zendesk opened an office in Singapore. [7] As of 2011 &#91;update&#93; half of Zendesk''s customers were outside the United States. [5] Although the company was not yet profitable, Zendesk''s revenues grew five-fold from 2010 to 2012. [5] Revenues grew from $38 million in 2012 to $72 million in 2013. [8] Zendesk introduced its first app marketplace for third-party Zendesk software in 2012. [3] By 2013, the company had 450 employees. [3] In 2014, Zendesk filed an initial public offering [9] and was valued at $1.7 billion. [8] The IPO raised $100 million. [10] That same year, Zendesk announced its first acquisition. [11] It bought a live-chat company called Zopim for $29.8 million. [11] It acquired a French analytics company called BIME for $45 million. [12] It also acquired Outbound.io, which developed software to manage direct communications with customers on social media sites. [13] Additionally, Zendesk acquired Base, who developed sales automation software that competed with Salesforce , for $50 million. [14] In 2015, Zendesk filed oppositions at the United States Patent and Trademark Office to 49 trademarks including the word "zen". The Electronic Frontier Foundation characterized its approach of filing multiple trademark infringement lawsuits covering a w', '["clm_wiki_zendesk_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
