VALUES
  ('cs_pandora_operations', 'org_pandora', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Works and Days Pandora by John William Waterhouse , 1896 The more famous version of the Pandora myth comes from another of Hesiod''s poems, Works and Days . In this version of the myth (lines 60–105), [1] Hesiod expands upon her origin and moreover widens the scope of the misery she inflicts on humanity. As before, she is created by Hephaestus, but now more gods contribute to her completion (63–82): Athena taught her needlework and weaving (63–4); Aphrodite "shed grace upon her head and cruel longing and cares that weary the limbs" (65–6); Hermes gave her "a shameless mind and a deceitful nature" (67–8); Hermes also gave her the power of speech, putting in her "lies and crafty words" (77–80); Athena then clothed her (72); next Persuasion and the Charites adorned her with necklaces and other finery (72–4); the Horae adorned her with a garland crown (75). Finally, Hermes gives this woman a name: "Pandora [i.e. "All-Gift"], because all they who dwelt on Olympus gave each a gift, a plague to men who eat bread" (81–2). [2] In this retelling of her story, Pandora''s deceitful feminine nature becomes the least of humanity''s worries. For she brings with her a jar (which, due to textual corruption in the sixteenth century, came to be called a box) [3] [4] [5] containing "countless plagues" (100). Prometheus had (fearing further reprisals) warned his brother Epimetheus not to accept any gifts from Zeus. But Epimetheus did not listen; he accepted Pandora, who promptly scattered the contents of her jar. As a result, Hesiod tells us, the earth and sea are "full of evils" (101). One item, however, did not escape the jar (96–9): Only Hope remained there in an unbreakable home within under the rim of the great jar, and did not fly out at the door; for ere that, the lid of the jar stopped her, by the will of Aegis-holding Zeus who gathers the clouds. Hesiod does not say why Hope ( Elpis ) remained in the jar. [6] Hesiod closes with a moral (105): there is "no way to escape the will of', '["clm_wiki_pandora_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 1005. Westlake Corporation (slug=westlake, wiki='Westlake Corporation')
