VALUES
  ('cs_seagate_history', 'org_seagate', 'overview', 'History',
   'American data storage company Seagate Technology Holdings plc Operational headquarters in Fremont, California Type Public Traded as Nasdaq : STX Nasdaq-100 component S&amp;P 500 component Industry Computer storage Predecessor Shugart Technology Founded November 1, 1979 &#59;&#32;46 years ago ( 1979-11-01 ) (as Shugart Technology) Founders Alan Shugart Tom Mitchell Doug Mahon Finis Conner Syed Iftikar Headquarters Fremont, California , United States (operational) Dublin , Ireland (legal domicile) Area served Worldwide Key people Dave Mosley ( chairman &amp; CEO ) Products Hard disk drives hybrid drives solid-state drives Revenue US$ 9.10 billion (2025) Operating income US$1.89 billion (2025) Net income US$1.47 billion (2025) Total assets US$8.02 billion (2025) Total equity − US$453 million (2025) Number of employees 30,000 (2025) Subsidiaries LaCie Website seagate Footnotes Financials as of June 27,&#32;2025 &#91;update&#93; . [1] Seagate Technology Holdings plc is an Irish-domiciled data storage company. It was incorporated in 1978 as Shugart Technology and commenced business in 1979. [2] Since 2010, the company has been incorporated in Dublin , Ireland, with operational headquarters in Fremont, California , United States. Seagate developed the first 5.25-inch hard disk drive (HDD), the 5-megabyte ST-506 , in 1980. They were a major supplier in the microcomputer market during the 1980s, especially after the introduction of the IBM XT in 1983. Much of its growth has come through its acquisition of competitors. In 1989, Seagate acquired Control Data Corporation ''s Imprimis division, the makers of CDC''s HDD products. Seagate acquired Conner Peripherals in 1996, Maxtor in 2006, and Samsung ''s HDD business in 2011. Today, Seagate, along with its competitor Western Digital , dominates the HDD market. Seagate Technology operates massive, vertically integrated manufacturing and R&amp;D operations in Thailand , serving as a critical global hub that accounts for nearly 50% of the company''s worldwide workforce with around 14,000 to 15,000 employees across facilities in Teparuk (Samut Prakan) and Korat (Nakhon Ratchasima). ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Seagate Technology Holdings plc. FY 2025 Annual Report (Form 10-K)" . U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission . August 1, 2025. ^ Cuff, Daniel F. (August 9, 1983). "Business People; Seagate Technology Names New President" . The New York Times . Archived from the original on November 15, 2019 . Retrieved May 8, 2018 .', '["clm_wiki_seagate_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   20, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_seagate_leadership', 'org_seagate', 'overview', 'Leadership',
   'Founding as Shugart Technology Seagate Technology (then called Shugart Technology ) was incorporated on November 1, 1978, and commenced operations with co-founders Al Shugart , Tom Mitchell, Doug Mahon, Finis Conner , and Syed Iftikar in October 1979. [1] The company came into being when Conner approached Shugart with the idea of starting a new company to develop 5.25-inch HDDs which Conner predicted would be a coming economic boom in the disk drive market. [2] To avoid a lawsuit from Xerox ''s subsidiary Shugart Associates (also founded by Shugart), the name was changed to Seagate Technology while retaining the letters S, G and T from Shugart. [3] ^ Seagate Technology Prospectus, September 24, 1981, p.4 ^ Cite error: The named reference Burton was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ body:not( ):not( ) "Oral History of Syed Iftikar" (PDF) . Computer History Museum. 2006. p. 17. Archived (PDF) from the original on December 27, 2014 . Retrieved March 9, 2013 .', '["clm_wiki_seagate_leadership"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_seagate_operations', 'org_seagate', 'overview', 'Operations',
   'Dave Mosley era (2017–present) On July 25, 2017, David "Dave" Mosley was appointed CEO, effective from October 1, 2017 after longtime CEO, Steve Luczo stepped down and became executive chairman. [1] [2] In June 2018, Seagate was honored at the 14th Annual Manufacturing Leadership Awards Gala in Huntington Beach, California . [3] In 2018, Seagate invested in Series A and B of Ripple , an enterprise blockchain company . [4] In 2019, Seagate invested £47 million in a research and development project at its factory in Derry , Northern Ireland. [5] In 2020, Seagate announced that it was moving its headquarters and most of its staff from Cupertino to Fremont, California. [6] From May to June that year, the company laid off 500 employees across 12 countries due to a push for better operational efficiencies. Seagate planned for the rearrangement of more resources, including combining facilities in Minnesota . [7] In September 2020, Seagate announced it had entered the object storage business and introduced CORTX, an open-source object storage software, Lyve Rack, a reference architecture based on CORTX, and a corresponding developer community. The community is a group of open-source researchers and developers working to advance mass-capacity object storage. [8] [9] CORTX open-source software is hosted for download and collaboration on GitHub . [10] As of May 18, 2021, the new Irish public limited company Seagate Technology Holdings plc became the publicly traded parent company of Seagate, replacing "Seagate Technology plc." [11] In November 2021, at the Open Compute Summit , Seagate demonstrated the industry''s first HDD with a non-volatile memory express (NVMe) interface. [12] This was unusual because HDDs operate far below the capabilities of the NVMe interface, which is usually associated with faster storage media like SSDs. In May 2022, Seagate presented and demonstrated its LiDAR system at the Autosens conference in Detroit. [13] In February 2023, it divested its LiDAR ', '["clm_wiki_seagate_operations"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'orgs_sections_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 1067. Micron Technology (slug=micron, wiki='Micron Technology')
